    <!-- Detect Stats tooltips -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.Run">Shortlist likely player stats (HP/MP/Gold/…) and confirm each against the live game — find a live instance and read its current value. Reference only; verify before use.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.UseSnapshot">Cross-reference your two most-recent snapshots and boost fields whose value DECREASED. Workflow: capture a snapshot, take damage / spend in-game, capture again, then Detect. Needs ≥2 usable snapshots.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.Filter">Substring filter over the property, class and category columns (case-insensitive).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.GWorld">Locate a live instance of this class in the GWorld object graph (opens Live Walker, where you can Copy CE Field).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.Finder">Find all live instances of this class in the Instance Finder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Detect.Copy">Copy the property name to the clipboard.</sys:String>

    <!-- Dump Explorer panel — offline browser over a "Dump All" .jsonl file -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Load">📂 Load .jsonl</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.LoadLast">⤓ Last export</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Recheck">🔄 Re-check live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Cancel">Cancel</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.SearchHint">Keyword — searches class / property / function names at once (space = AND)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.MetaNote">ⓘ Rows are class definitions (metadata), not live instances — a class isn't part of the GWorld/GameEngine object graph, so there's no Locate here. 🡒 Jump opens the class; 🔍 Instances finds a live instance you can then Locate via Related.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Cat.All">All</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Cat.Class">Classes</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Cat.Property">Properties</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Cat.Function">Functions</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColKind">Kind</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColName">Name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColOwner">Owner class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColType">Type / signature</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColOffset">Offset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.ColPath">Class path</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.Jump">🡒 Jump</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.FindInstances">🔍 Instances</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Dump.CopyPath">copy path</sys:String>
    <!-- Dump Explorer tooltips -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.Load">Open a “Dump All” .jsonl file (Export ▸ Dump All) and browse every class, property and function in one searchable list — no game connection required.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.LoadLast">Load the most recent Export ▸ Dump All from this session (no file dialog). Disabled until you export one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.Recheck">Scan the connected game's live classes and re-split results into “in current game” (matched by class name, restart-safe) vs. not. Requires a connected, scanned game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.Category">Limit results to a single kind (classes / properties / functions), or show all.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.Jump">Open the owning class's live object in the Live Walker.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.FindInstances">Find live instances of the owning class in the Instance Finder — the bridge to Related ▸ Locate in GWorld/GameEngine (which work on instances, not class definitions).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Dump.CopyPath">Copy the class object path to the clipboard.</sys:String>

    <!-- Related Objects panel -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.AddressHint">UObject / actor address (hex) — or hand off from another tab</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.Load">Load</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.DetectTarget">🎯 Detect target</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.CandidatesHeader">Target candidates (best first — click one to inspect):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.ColRelation">Relation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.ColObject">Object</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.ColField">Field</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.ColAddress">Address</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.ShowButton">🔗 Related</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Related.IconButton">🔗</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.Show">Show this object's related objects — components, GAS AbilitySystemComponent → AttributeSets, Controller↔Pawn — in the Related tab</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.Load">List this object's class/outer, its Controller↔Pawn counterpart, and the sub-objects it owns (components, GAS AbilitySystemComponent → AttributeSets)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.DetectTarget">Auto-detect the actor the player is currently targeting (GWorld → PlayerController → Pawn → target field) and load its related objects — no class-name guessing</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.GWorld">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this object and show it in Live Walker</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.GameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this object. Reaches engine / GameInstance / LocalPlayer / engine-subsystem objects the GWorld graph can't — but NOT most world actors (use Locate in GWorld for those)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.Walk">Open this object in Live Walker for field-by-field inspection</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.Find">Find all live instances of this object's class (Instance Finder)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Related.Copy">Copy this object's address to the clipboard</sys:String>

    <!-- Value Search -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Banner">Native C++ fields (non-UPROPERTY) cannot be found here — even if they live inside a UObject's memory. Enable "Native-C" below to scan those raw. Use Cheat Engine's raw memory scan for full control.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.NativeBanner">Native-C scan ON: also reading raw, unmanaged (non-UPROPERTY) bytes inside each object via the Guess-What heuristic. First scan is intentionally noisy — narrow with Game-classes-only / Newest-first, then converge with Next Scan (Changed / Unchanged / Increased / Decreased). Numeric types only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.DataType">Type:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.ScanType">Scan:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Value">Value:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Value2">… to</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Rounding">Rounding:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Rounding">How a fractional float/double target is reduced to the integer the game DISPLAYS before compare. Round (default) = nearest (10.9 → 11); Trunc = toward zero (10.9 → 10); Ceil = up (10.1 → 11). Float/double field values are used as-is; the mode reaches integer fields only when a fractional target/bound is coerced.</sys:String>
    <!-- Rounding-mode label reused by the Snapshot + SPC panels (same picker; the
         per-panel hint TextBlock carries the explanation, so no separate tooltip). -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Rounding">Rounding:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Rounding">Rounding:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.GameOnly">Game classes only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Parallel">Parallel scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.BatchRead">Batch read</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Deep">Deep (nested containers)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Deep">Opt-in (default off). Also scans deeply-nested containers — struct arrays, struct-valued maps, and nested TArray/TSet — so a value buried inside e.g. SaveSlotList[1].MsTuneData.MsTunes[0].WeaponTuneList[0].Tunes[N] is found. Heavier per object, so enable only when a shallow scan misses the value. In Group mode each numeric array / struct-array element becomes its own match block, so the group is matched WITHIN one array.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.NativeC">Native-C (raw)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.NativeC">Opt-in (default off). In addition to reflected UPROPERTY fields, scans each object's UNMANAGED holes — the byte ranges within the object (from after the UObject header up to its class size) that no property covers — for your value, interpreting the raw bytes at your chosen width. This finds native, non-UPROPERTY C++ members such as HP/MP that reflection can't see. Numeric data types only (ignored for string/vector/bool). A raw FIRST scan is deliberately noisy (any matching 4 bytes is a hit) — it is meant to be NARROWED then converged with Next Scan. Enabling this auto-checks "Newest first"; matched rows show Origin = "Native-C (Type)". With "Deep" ALSO enabled, the raw-hole scan additionally reaches INSIDE struct-array elements (e.g. a value inside a TArray of a USTRUCT that has no UPROPERTY members, which neither reflection nor the object-body native scan can see) — heavier and noisier, so pair with distinctive values + Next Scan.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.NativeC.Group">Opt-in (default off, Group mode). In addition to reflected fields, folds each object's UNMANAGED holes (non-UPROPERTY raw bytes) into its group block, so a group whose values include a native C++ member (HP/MP) still matches — the values must co-occur at distinct offsets within ONE object. Object block only (never the deep-container blocks); bounded to ≤64 raw leaves per object. Most selective with DISTINCT values; small common values (0/1/100) make raw matching noisy — prefer distinctive values, then converge with Next Scan. Numeric slots only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.AutoSkipNoise">Skip Engine/System noise</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.AutoSkipNoise">Opt-in (default off). A looser PRE-filter that skips pure engine/system classes (UI widgets, textures, sounds, particle/Niagara systems, anim instances, and /Script engine packages) at the SOURCE — their instances never enter the result set, so the scan is faster and the candidate list is cleaner. This differs from the class picker below the results, which filters AFTER the scan by exact class name. A gameplay guardrail force-keeps any Actor / Pawn / Character / ActorComponent / Controller / PlayerState / GameInstance-derived class, so a player Pawn's X/Y/Z (and HP/MP living in components or GAS AttributeSets) is NEVER skipped. Mirrors Snapshot's "Auto detect Engine/System noise". Locked at First Scan — toggle then re-scan to change it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.NewestFirst">Newest first</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.NewestFirst">Walk objects newest-first (most-recently-allocated first) so that when results hit the cap, the survivors are the newest instances rather than low-index templates/defaults (CDOs). WHEN TO USE: for values on a just-spawned actor, or on temporary combat "shadow" objects a game spawns on encounter-start and writes HP/MP/EXP back from after the fight — these sit at HIGH indices and a normal (oldest-first) scan would truncate them away. WHEN TO UNCHECK: if the value lives on your operated character that is created early at game start and persists, it sits at a LOW index — uncheck this so it isn't truncated. Auto-checked when Native-C is enabled (because raw first-scans get truncated); auto-cleared when Native-C is turned off; usable on its own for reflected scans too.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.CrossObject">Cross-object (owned components)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.CrossObject">Opt-in (default off, Group mode). Also folds in the numeric properties of the sub-objects each actor OWNS — its components and (for GAS games) its AbilitySystemComponent's AttributeSets — into the actor's match block, so a group whose values are split across {actor, components, attribute sets} is found (e.g. Health on the pawn + Gold on an InventoryComponent). Ownership-driven: only sub-objects whose Outer chains back to the actor are followed; selectivity comes from matching all N values, not from a class-name filter. A per-slot row's field name shows the path (e.g. HealthComp.CurrentHealth) and its handoffs open the owning sub-object.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.MaxResults">Max:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.MaxResults">Upper bound on candidates returned by a scan, counted across ALL objects and TArray elements combined (not per array). When the limit is hit the scan stops early and the status row shows a truncation note — raise this, or narrow the value/predicate, to see the full set. Inside a single array every element (index 0..Num-1) is scanned — there is no per-array cap; only arrays with a corrupt/absurd length (over 10,000,000 elements) are skipped as a safety guard. Scans also bail after the Timeout deadline (10–60s, default 15 — see the Timeout slider).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Timeout">Timeout:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.PerSlotCap">Leaves/slot:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Group.Matched">ONE example pairing, not the whole match.&#x0a;&#x0a;An object usually satisfies the group in many ways at once; this column shows a single witness, preferring fields from the SAME struct (Health.CurrentValue beside Health.BaseValue) and, when a filter is active, the field that matched it.&#x0a;&#x0a;"(+35)" after a value means 35 OTHER fields on this object also matched that slot — they are matched, just not displayed here. Select the row and press "All fields" to list them by name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.AllFields">All fields</sys:String>
    <!-- Snapshot Group / SPC Group show the same "(+N)" annotation from their own
         offset lists, but have no live session and so no "All fields" button — this
         tooltip must therefore explain the annotation WITHOUT pointing at a control
         those panels do not have. -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Group.MatchedOffline">ONE example pairing, not the whole match.&#x0a;&#x0a;An object usually satisfies the group in several ways at once; this column shows a single witness per value, preferring fields from the same struct.&#x0a;&#x0a;"(+35)" after a value means 35 OTHER fields on this object also matched it — they are matched, just not displayed. Expand the row to see each value's matching-field count; to list them by name, run the same group in Value Search, which can query the live object.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Group.AllFields">List every field on this object that matched THIS value, by name — not just the one the row displays. Press again to collapse.&#x0a;&#x0a;A row can only show one pairing, so a field that matched can look like it was missed: a Changed + Unchanged scan may keep {Health.CurrentValue, TickCount} for the first value and 36 fields including FrozenInt for the second, then display the Health pair. Both pairings are real; the row has room for one. Each listed field carries the same Live / Addr / Pivot / Locate actions as the row itself.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.PerSlotCap">How many matching fields to KEEP per value on each object (group scan only, 8-4096, default 256).&#x0a;&#x0a;This is not a speed setting. The kept list is what a later Changed / Decreased / Unchanged scan re-reads, and fields are collected base-class-first — so a small value silently hides an object's OWN fields behind the ones it inherits.&#x0a;&#x0a;Raise it only for objects with unusually many numeric fields; the scan warns in the log when it has to truncate.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Mode">Switch between Single value (scan one value at a time, CE-style) and Group (find objects holding ALL of 2–4 values at distinct offsets — far more selective for pinning down a specific actor).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Timeout">Maximum seconds a First / Group scan may run before it bails early and returns whatever matched so far (the status row notes the truncation). Range 10–90s, default 25. Raise it for huge games (400K+ objects) that keep hitting the deadline; lower it for snappier scans on small games. Applies to both Single and Group First Scan; Next Scan (refine) re-reads only the existing candidates and is already fast.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.FirstScan">First Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.NextScan">Next Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.NewScan">New Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.OpenLive">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.OpenInst">inst</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.OpenInst">Open this hit's owning class in the Instance Finder tab — pre-fills the class name and runs the search.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.CopyAddr">Addr</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.PivotThis">📊 Pivot</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.LocateGWorld">🌍</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this value's owning object and show it in Live Walker</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.LocateGameEngine">⚙</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this value's owning object and show it in Live Walker. Reaches engine / GameInstance / LocalPlayer objects the GWorld graph can't — but for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.CaseSensitive">Case sensitive</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Filter">Filter:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.FilterWatermark">Filter the full result set (server-side) — any column, case-insensitive…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Sort">Sort:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.SortDesc">Desc</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.LoadMore">Load More</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Sort">Filter and sort run in the DLL over the WHOLE result set; the grid shows one page at a time. Load More fetches the next page. Clicking a column header sorts by that column (server-side) and updates this picker; click again to flip direction.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Cancel">Cancel</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Cancel">Cancel the in-flight scan. The UI stops waiting immediately; the DLL self-terminates the scan at its deadline (usually sub-second).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.CaseSensitive">When checked, string scans (FString / FName / FText) match case exactly. Default unchecked = CE-style case-insensitive matching. Only affects string DataTypes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Parallel">When checked (default), the First Scan walks all objects using multiple worker threads — much faster. Uncheck to force a single-threaded scan: slower, but it avoids the burst of concurrent cross-thread memory reads that some games' anti-tamper / anti-cheat can flag. Only the First Scan parallelizes; Next Scan (refine) is already single-threaded.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.BatchRead">When checked (default), each object's fixed-width fields are read in one body read instead of one read per field — fewer reads and better cache locality, so the First Scan is faster. The DLL automatically falls back to per-field reads when it isn't worthwhile or a read fails. Uncheck to always read one field at a time (reads only the exact bytes each field needs). Strings and array/set/map data are always read directly. First Scan only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.DataType">Pick the UE property type to scan. NumericNoByte scans every word/dword/qword/float/double field in one pass and compares the value against each field using its own declared width (no need to know whether it's stored as int or float) — 1-byte and bool fields are excluded so small values don't flood the results. NumericAll is the same one-pass scan but ALSO includes 1-byte fields (Int8/UInt8) — handy when the value really is a byte, but small values match a huge number of fields. Single numeric primitives, FString / FName / FText, and FVector / FRotator are also supported. FTransform is reserved but currently returns no hits. The scan also walks TArray&lt;T&gt; containers of the chosen inner type — every element (index 0..Num-1) is scanned and matches appear as "FieldName[index]" rows (arrays over 10,000,000 elements are skipped as a corruption guard). See the Max tooltip for the global result cap.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.FirstScan">Walk every UPROPERTY field of the chosen type across all UObject instances (including TArray elements of that type), apply the predicate, and start a session for Next Scan refines. For strings use Contains / StartsWith / EndsWith. For vectors enter "X,Y,Z".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.NextScan">Re-read each candidate and prune by the new predicate. Changed / Unchanged / Increased / Decreased compare against the previous round.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.NewScan">End the current session and reset for a fresh scan.</sys:String>
    <!-- Multiple values group scan (build 1276) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Mode.Single">Single value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Mode.Group">Multiple values (group)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.Hint">Find objects that hold ALL of these values at once (e.g. Str + Def + Dex + Int in the same stats object). Order doesn't matter; far more selective than searching one value. Enter 2–4 values.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.AddRow">+ value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.RemoveRow">− value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.FirstScan">Group First Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.NextScan">Group Next Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.NewScan">New Group Scan</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.VS.Group.FilterWatermark">Filter matched objects — class / instance / field / value. Space = AND, e.g. "tickcount frozenint"…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Group.Filter">Server-side filter over the whole result set. **Space = AND**: each term must match something on the object, but they may match DIFFERENT fields.&#x0a;&#x0a;This is also how you ask for a SPECIFIC pairing. A row shows one pairing out of the many an object satisfies — a Changed + Unchanged scan can hold 2 x 36 = 72 of them, and nothing in the data says which one you meant. Naming both fields is the request: type "tickcount frozenint" and the row becomes TickCount beside FrozenInt instead of whichever pair came first.&#x0a;&#x0a;One term still works as before, and matches class / instance / field name / value.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Group.FirstScan">Scan every object for one that simultaneously holds all of the entered values at distinct numeric-property offsets (in any order). Each value defaults to NumericNoByte (matches int16/int32/int64/float/double widths). Use Group Next Scan with the new values to narrow and lock each field's offset.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.VS.Group.ScanType">Per-value match predicate. First Scan: Exact / Bigger / Smaller (compares against the value). Next Scan also allows Changed / Unchanged / Increased / Decreased — these compare against the previous round instead of a value (the value box hides), so you can find objects where the values moved a known way without knowing the exact numbers.</sys:String>

