    <sys:String x:Key="str.Toolbar.AobMakerRefresh">&#x27F3;</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Toolbar.AobMakerRefresh">Re-check whether Cheat Engine is running with the AOBMaker plugin loaded. Navigation/record buttons also re-probe on use, so this is just for an at-a-glance status.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Button">Run Self-Test</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Hint">Auto-picks a KismetMathLibrary helper, invokes it via static-native fast path, and verifies the return value. Confirms the ProcessEvent hook is on the correct vtable slot.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Running">Probing KismetMathLibrary helpers...</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.NoCandidate">No testable BlueprintFunctionLibrary helper found in this game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Error">Self-Test failed: {0}</sys:String>
    <!-- Self-Test FAILED. {0} label, {1} expected, {2} actual, {3} advice (one of the four below), {4} raw buffer. -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail">✗ {0} expected {1}, got {2}&#10;{3}&#10;Raw buffer: {4}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail.HookFiring">The ProcessEvent hook is installed AND firing, so the invoke path itself is working. A wrong answer here most likely means this game's build resolves that helper through BlueprintFastCall, which makes it a no-op when called via ProcessEvent — so this result is not evidence of a broken hook. Verify with a game-specific UFunction instead. Re-deploying the DLL will not change it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail.HookNeverFired">The ProcessEvent hook is installed but has never fired. That is the signature of a MIS-DETECTED vtable slot — check init-*.log for 'VALIDATION FAILED'. Re-deploying the DLL will NOT help: the binary is current, the detected slot is wrong. The same reading also appears when the game thread is simply idle (paused, loading, minimised), so retry once while the game is actually running before concluding.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail.NotDispatched">The DLL REFUSED this call — it never reached ProcessEvent, so the untouched return slot says nothing about the function. Check init-*.log: 'VALIDATION FAILED' means a detected vtable slot was rejected because the hook never fired (re-deploying the DLL will NOT fix that), and 'no UObject vtable available yet' means no scan has run in this process — run one and retry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail.HookOff">The ProcessEvent hook is not installed, so this call used the direct fallback. Check init-*.log for 'DetectProcessEvent' and 'VALIDATION FAILED': "no UObject vtable available yet" means run a scan first and retry; "VALIDATION FAILED" means a detected slot was rejected because it never fired, and the hook has just been disabled for that reason; and a detection failure means re-deploying the DLL will NOT help — restart the game and re-inject.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.SelfTest.Fail.Unknown">The DLL's hook state could not be read, so the cause is unknown. It is either a mis-detected vtable slot (check init-*.log for 'VALIDATION FAILED' — re-deploying the DLL does not fix that) or a helper this game compiled to BlueprintFastCall, which no-ops through ProcessEvent even when the hook is correct.</sys:String>

    <!-- System tab: Pipe Activity (live tail of UI<->DLL pipe traffic) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.PipeLog.Header">Pipe Activity</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.PipeLog.Hint">Live tail of UI&#x2194;DLL pipe traffic, newest first. Columns: time · lane (I = interactive, B = bulk) · direction (&#x2192; sent, &#x2190; reply with round-trip ms, &#x26A1; event) · command · id. A convenience mirror of the pipe log file — watch interactive commands stay responsive while a heavy scan streams on the bulk lane.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.PipeLog.Pause">Pause</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.PipeLog.Clear">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.PipeLog.Empty">No pipe traffic yet — connect to a game and interact with a panel.</sys:String>

    <!-- System tab: Maintenance (open log folder + remove all snapshot databases) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Maintenance.Header">Maintenance</sys:String>

    <!-- Diagnostics (Sense) — what the pipe traffic costs -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Header">Diagnostics — DLL dispatch cost</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Refresh">Refresh</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Reset">Reset counters</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Auto">Auto (5s)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.AutoTip">Re-read every 5 seconds. Deliberately unhurried: each poll is itself a dispatch, so a faster timer would inflate the very numbers shown here (get_diagnostics already appears in its own table). It also makes CPU% meaningful, since that needs two samples to difference. Pauses automatically while you are on another tab and resumes when you come back.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.RefreshTip">Read a fresh snapshot: how long each pipe command has occupied the DLL's dispatcher, plus memory / CPU / thread counts and game-thread health. Read-only and safe to click repeatedly. CPU% needs two samples, so it shows a dash on the first read. NOTE: the memory, CPU, thread and handle figures are for the WHOLE GAME PROCESS — our DLL is injected into it and cannot be measured separately, so a multi-GB working set is the game's, not ours. Only the dispatch table above is purely our cost.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.ResetTip">Clear the counters and restart the uptime clock, so the next measurement covers one deliberate action instead of the whole session. Counters also reset automatically when the last client disconnects.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Summary">{0} dispatches over {1}s — dispatcher busy {2}% of wall-clock · GObjects {3:N0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Process">Game process (not the DLL): working set {0} · private {1} · CPU {2} · {3} threads · {4} handles</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.GameThread">Game thread: PE hook {0} · {1} · {2} · {3:N0} fires</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.LastFire">last fire {0:N0} ms ago</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.NeverFired">never fired (hook installs on first invoke)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Responsive">responsive</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Stalled">STALLED</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.NotConnected">Diagnostics need a live DLL connection.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Reset.Done">Counters reset.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.Diag.Error">Diagnostics failed: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.OpenLogFolder">Open Log Folder</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.OpenLogFolderTip">Open the application log folder (%LOCALAPPDATA%\UE5CEDumper\Logs) in the file browser.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.OpenLogFolder.Error">Could not open log folder: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs">Compress Logs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogsTip">Compress archived log files in place with NTFS LZX compression (measured 12.8:1 on a real 102 MB log folder). The files keep their name and extension and still open in any text editor or grep — nothing needs to be unpacked. Skipped: this session's own logs, files under 4 KB, files written in the last hour, files already compressed, and a game's newest log until it has been idle for 7 days. Safe to run while a game is playing: it runs at idle priority.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs.Running">Compressing logs…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs.Result">Compressed {0} log file(s): {1} MB → {2} MB on disk ({3} MB saved), {4} failed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs.NothingToDo">Nothing to compress — {0} already compressed, {1} skipped (too small, too recent, or in use).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs.Unsupported">Log compression needs an NTFS volume; this one is not.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.CompressLogs.Error">Log compression failed: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.AutoCompressLogs">Compress logs older than 7 days at startup</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.AutoCompressLogsTip">Off by default. When on, every launch runs the same compression in the background at idle priority over logs untouched for 7+ days. Retention is unaffected: compression does not change a file's timestamp, so the 21-day cleanup still deletes exactly what it would have.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots">Remove All Snapshot Data</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshotsTip">Permanently delete the snapshot database FILES for EVERY game (snapshots.*.db and their sidecars, in %LOCALAPPDATA%\UE5CEDumper\Snapshots) from disk — not a row purge. Files currently in use by an active capture are skipped. Bookmarks are not touched. Experimental feature; irreversible.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.ConfirmTitle">Remove all snapshot data</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.ConfirmMessage">Permanently delete ALL snapshot database files for EVERY game? This deletes the files from disk and cannot be undone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.ConfirmYes">Delete Files</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.None">No snapshot database files found.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.Result">Removed {0} snapshot database file(s).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.ResultPartial">Removed {0} file(s); {1} still in use — stop any active capture and retry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.System.RemoveAllSnapshots.Error">Error: {0}</sys:String>

    <!-- Credit footer (System tab + Live Walker empty state) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Credit.AobMakerPrefix">AOBMaker: </sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Credit.AobMakerUrl">https://github.com/bbfox0703/AOBMaker-Release</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Credit.RepoPrefix">Repository: </sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Credit.RepoUrl">https://github.com/bbfox0703/UE5CEDumper</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Credit.OpenInBrowserTip">Open in default browser</sys:String>

    <!-- Experimental features gate (System-tab credit checkbox) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Experimental.EnableHint">Enable advanced experimental features</sys:String>

    <!-- Snapshot tab (experimental — Phase A) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Intro">Capture every numeric UPROPERTY of every (scoped) object into a persisted snapshot. Snapshots feed SPC Query and Class Pivot. Enable "Native-C" to also capture unmanaged (non-UPROPERTY) raw values.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.NativeC">Native-C (raw)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.NativeC">Opt-in (default off). In addition to reflected UPROPERTY fields, captures each object's UNMANAGED holes (non-UPROPERTY raw bytes) as synthetic "&lt;raw@0xNN&gt;" fields — the bytes are interpreted via the Guess-What heuristic and normalized to a numeric type (pointer/padding guesses are dropped). Lets SPC Query diff and Class Pivot track native values (HP/MP) that reflection can't see. Slower and larger per capture. Raw fields join across snapshots by offset; a guess that flips type between captures (rare for stable values) simply won't join.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.CaptureHeader">Capture snapshot</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.CompareHeader">Compare snapshots (diff)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.NotConnected">Connect to a game to capture snapshots.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Scope">Scope:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Family">Type:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Family">Type-family narrowing applied ON TOP of Scope. "All numeric" captures every eligible width; "Integers only" keeps Int8/16/32/64 (+ unsigned) and drops Float/Double; "Floats only" keeps Float/Double and drops every integer. A source-level cut that shrinks the snapshot DB when the hunt is type-specific — floats for HP/MP/coordinates, integers for counts/flags/IDs. Lossy by design: re-capture to bring the other family back.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.MaxDataset">Max size:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.MaxDataset">Opt-in in-capture ceiling ("提前止血"). When the live database size crosses this, the capture stops early and KEEPS the partial snapshot (the first-N objects scanned). "Off" disables the cap. This is distinct from the per-game quota, which only evicts whole OLDER snapshots AFTER a capture finishes — it cannot bound a single oversized capture, so a huge game can balloon to many GB before any check. Use the cap (and Estimate size) on large games.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Estimate">Estimate size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Estimate">Pre-flight check: walks a small SAMPLE of objects (spread across the index space) with the current options, models the on-disk row cost, and extrapolates to the full object count — WITHOUT writing the database. Gives a "~X GB" go/no-go before committing to a multi-GB capture on a huge game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.GameOnly">Game objects only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.AutoSkipNoise">Auto detect Engine/System noise</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.AutoSkipNoise">On by default. Skips pure engine/system classes (UI widgets, textures, sounds, particle/Niagara systems, anim instances, and /Script engine packages) at the SOURCE — they never enter the snapshot, so capture is faster and the DB is smaller. This differs from the Noise picker below, which filters the finished snapshot AFTER capture. Source-level skipping is irreversible: re-capture (with this off) to bring a skipped class back. A gameplay guardrail force-keeps any Actor / Pawn / Character / ActorComponent / Controller / PlayerState-derived class, so a player Pawn's X/Y/Z (and HP/MP living in components or GAS AttributeSets) is never dropped. Mirrors the auto-detect already used in Value Search and the Instance finder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Label">Label:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.LabelWatermark">(optional — defaults to a timestamp)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Capture">Capture Snapshot</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Cancel">Cancel</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Refresh">Refresh</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Delete">Delete Selected</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.DeleteAll">Delete All</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.DeleteAllTip">Delete EVERY snapshot for this game and reclaim the disk (truncate + VACUUM). Irreversible.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.OpenDbFolder">Open DB folder</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.OpenDbFolderTip">Reveal this game's snapshot database (%LOCALAPPDATA%\UE5CEDumper\Snapshots\snapshots.&lt;pe_hash&gt;.db) in the file browser. A game you have not connected to for 21 days has its database deleted at startup to reclaim the disk.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Quota">Per-game quota:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.QuotaHint">When a capture exceeds the quota, the oldest snapshots of this game are dropped automatically.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Usage">Used:</sys:String>
    <!-- Free-disk-space guard (blocks ALL captures) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.MinFreeDisk">Min free disk:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.MinFreeDisk">Free-space safety net. Before ANY snapshot capture (manual or auto), the drive holding the snapshot database must have at least this much free space, otherwise the capture is refused. The requirement is the SMALLER of the two: a percentage of the whole drive OR an absolute floor in GB (e.g. a 1 TB drive at 10% / 50 GB → needs 50 GB free; a 200 GB drive → needs 20 GB). Set the percent to 0 to rely on the GB floor alone.</sys:String>
    <!-- Auto snapshot (periodic capture) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Header">Auto snapshot</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.On">On</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Off">Off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Interval">Interval (sec):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Auto.Interval">Target time between the START of one auto snapshot and the next (default 900 s = 15 min, minimum 60 s). If a capture runs longer than the interval, the interval auto-extends — there is always at least a 60 s idle gap between the end of one snapshot and the start of the next, which also gives the game a breather (auto snapshot deliberately avoids lowering scan thread priority, which would starve the capture when the game is busy).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Retention">Retention:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Auto.Retention">KeepRecent — capture forever, keeping only the newest N snapshots (older ones are dropped). FixedCount — capture exactly N times this session, then stop (all N are kept, still subject to the quota).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Count">Count (N):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.AdjustQuota">Auto-adjust quota</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Auto.AdjustQuota">Off (default): total usage stays bound by the per-game quota; if the quota can only hold one snapshot while you want to keep more, auto snapshot stops. On: when the quota can't hold the wanted number of snapshots, it is raised to the next preset that fits (up to Unlimited). The free-disk-space guard is always the real ceiling regardless.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Auto.Hint">Periodically capture snapshots unattended. This is a manual switch — it never auto-starts on connect or auto-resumes on the next launch; only the settings are remembered. Use Cancel to stop a running auto snapshot.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Saved">Saved snapshots</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Size">Est. Size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Label">Label</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Captured">Captured (UTC)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Objects">Objects</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Fields">Fields</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Scope">Scope</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Col.Ue">UE</sys:String>
    <!-- Snapshot diff -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Old">Old:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.New">New:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Run">Run Diff</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.DeepNote">Diff is always deep</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Diff.DeepNote">Snapshot Diff always includes nested array / struct-array element changes (e.g. SupportActionGauge[3], shown as Array[N]) — no Deep toggle is needed here. The Deep toggle exists only in Group mode, where it opts array elements in as group-match slots.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.ClassFilter">Class:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.PropFilter">Field:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Dir">Change:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.CopyAddr">Addr</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.OpenWalker">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.LocateGWorld">🌍</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Diff.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this row's owning object and show it in Live Walker, scrolled to the changed field (in-session diff only)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.LocateGameEngine">⚙</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Diff.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this row's owning object, shown in Live Walker scrolled to the changed field (in-session diff only). Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.ObjectFilter">Object:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Global">Filter all:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.GlobalHint">match any column…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.OldRange">Old:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.NewRange">New:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.ApplyRange">Apply range</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.ResetRange">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Hint">Diff joins fields by object identity within a session (same game launch). Old &amp; New should be from the same play session.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.Change">Δ</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.Class">Class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.Object">Object</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.Field">Field</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.Old">Old</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Diff.Col.New">New</sys:String>

    <!-- Snapshot Group Match (Multiple Values over a captured snapshot) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.ModeLabel">Mode:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.ModeDiff">Diff</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.ModeGroup">Group (Multiple Values)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Snapshot">Snapshot:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Find">Find</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Clear">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.AddRow">+ value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.RemoveRow">− value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Deep">Deep (nested containers)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Group.Deep">Also match values inside NESTED containers / struct-array elements (e.g. a deeply-nested SaveSlotList[1]…Tunes[2]), folded into the owning object's block. Needed when the value you're after lives in an array/map, not a direct field. Off = direct fields only (faster, less noise).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.ValueHint">value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Value2Hint">to</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.CompareWith">Compare with:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Group.CompareWith">Pick an OLDER snapshot to compare against (Mode B). Enables the relative predicates (Changed / Unchanged / Increased / Decreased) — e.g. find an object whose Current HP decreased while Max HP stayed unchanged. Leave empty for absolute single-snapshot matching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.CompareClear">✕</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Snapshot.Group.CompareClear">Clear the compare snapshot — back to single-snapshot (absolute) matching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Hint">Find objects holding ALL of 2–4 values at distinct numeric offsets. Leave "Compare with" empty to match absolute values in one snapshot; pick an older snapshot there to compare across time, then Increased / Decreased / Unchanged work (e.g. "Current HP↓ + Max HP unchanged").</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Col.Class">Class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Col.Object">Object</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.Col.Matched">Matched values</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.OpenWalker">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.CopyAddr">Addr</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.LocateGWorld">🌍</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Snapshot.Group.LocateGameEngine">⚙</sys:String>

    <!-- SPC Query (experimental) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Intro">Find fields whose value SEQUENCE across snapshots matches a direction chain — no type, no value. Pick two or more snapshots (they may span game sessions), set how each should compare to the previous one (Increased / Decreased / Unchanged / Changed), and run. This is CE's "Unknown Initial Value + Increased/Decreased" generalised across persisted snapshots and restarts.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Join">Join:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.JoinHint">Strict = same class + normalised path + offset (fewest false positives). Loose = class + outer chain + name (recovers some spawned objects across sessions). In-session = same game launch only (exact).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ClassFilter">Class:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.PropFilter">Field:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResClass">Class:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResField">Field:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResObject">Object:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResGlobal">Filter all:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResGlobalHint">match any column…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.SeqFirst">First:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.SeqLast">Last:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ApplyRange">Apply range</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.ResetRange">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Run">Run SPC</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Refresh">Refresh</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.OpenWalker">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.LocateGWorld">🌍</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Spc.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this row's owning object and show it in Live Walker, scrolled to the changed field</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.LocateGameEngine">⚙</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Spc.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this row's owning object, shown in Live Walker scrolled to the changed field. Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.CopyAddr">Addr</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.PickerHeader">Snapshot sequence</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.BaselineHint">The oldest ticked snapshot is the baseline (its predicate is ignored). Each later snapshot's predicate compares it to the previous ticked one. Hit → Copy Address / Open in Live Walker hands the newest snapshot's live address to CE.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Spc.Col.Use">Use</sys:String>
