    <sys:String x:Key="str.IF.CopyAaScript">AA(B)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IF.CopyName">Name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.Load">Scan all UFunctions across all loaded UClasses. Takes 2-10s on large games (1M+ objects). Score is computed client-side from name keywords + class bonus + flag bonus.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.GameOnly">Skip /Script/Engine, /Script/CoreUObject, etc. Typically reduces the result set ~5x for shipping games. Recheck + re-Load to refresh.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.Filter">Substring match against function name OR class name (case-insensitive). e.g. "Money" or "PlayerCharacter".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.ClearFilters">Clear the name filter, category, and Show-all toggle (reset to defaults).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.OpenInst">Open this row's class in the Instance Finder tab — pre-fills the class name and runs the search to list its live instances.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.Category">Filter by category. Categories are picked from the strongest-scoring keyword bucket per function (Stats &gt; Inventory &gt; Movement &gt; Combat &gt; Utility tied with same score).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.ShowAll">When off, hide rows with score below the InterestingThreshold (currently 5). When on, show every UFunction regardless of score.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.GameplayActions">Opt-in (default off). Fold an extra keyword pack into scoring for character-control + world-interaction + shop verbs the default buckets skip — Dash / Dodge / Roll / Slide / Climb / Interact / Use / Open / Buy / Sell / Shop / Vendor / Merchant / Trade / Purchase. Lets a plain OpenShop() / Dash() / Interact() clear the threshold and show up (tip: combine with a name filter like "shop" or "buy"). Re-scores the loaded set in place — no reload needed. Noisier by design; turn off to return to the cheat-value default.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.CallableOnly">Filter (default off): show only functions flagged BlueprintCallable or Exec. Gameplay/control/shop entry points are almost always one of these two, and both flags survive cooking — so this hides native getters/setters/engine plumbing noise. Pairs well with "Gameplay Actions" + "Show All" to browse callable action functions. Pure display filter — doesn't change scores.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.Props">List the properties this function reads/writes (static Blueprint-bytecode scan). Native functions have no bytecode and show nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.OpenLive">Try to open this function in Live Walker. If the class has no live (non-CDO) instance, falls back to the Class Struct tab. Auto-expands the Functions section and selects the target row.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find a live instance of this function's class, then show the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find a live instance of this function's class, then show the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it). Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.CopyAaScript">Open the Copy AA Script (Baked) dialog for this function. Output requires ue5_invoke_helper.lua embedded in your CE table (Tools -&gt; Export CE Helper Lua File...).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.CopyName">Copy this function's name to the clipboard. Useful when pasting into a CE script or another tool.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IF.GenerateCT">📦 Generate CT</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.GenerateCT">Generate one .CT file containing baked-invoke entries for every selected row. Use Ctrl/Shift+click in the grid to multi-select first. Functions with parameters: the helper zero-fills PARAMS; edit the baked PARAMS table in the generated entry before activating.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IF.BatchProps">⤋ Props</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IF.BatchProps">Fill the "Uses" column for the selected rows at once (or all rows if none selected), so you see which class fields each function reads/writes without opening the per-row popup. Fast — each call reads only that one function's bytecode. Most rows show 0 (native funcs / no field access).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IF.UsesCol">Uses</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Common.CancelBatch">✕ Cancel</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Common.BatchFuncs">⤋ Funcs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Common.BatchFindFunc">⤋ Find Func</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Common.BatchFindFunc">Fill the "Funcs" column for the selected rows (or all currently-filtered rows if none selected): which UFunctions take each class as a parameter or return value (reflection scan — includes native functions). Each call is a full game-wide sweep, so narrow the filter or select rows first. Already-scanned rows are skipped (cached).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Common.FuncsCol">Funcs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Common.BatchFuncs">Fill the "Funcs" column for the selected rows (or all rows if none selected): which UFunctions reference each property (Blueprint bytecode xref). Each call is a full game scan, so this is slower than the function-side batch — select rows first (Ctrl/Shift+click) to scope it. Most rows show 0 (native-only access / unreferenced).</sys:String>

    <!-- Live Funcs (Live ProcessEvent Call Profiler) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.Start">● Start</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.Stop">■ Stop</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.Refresh">Refresh</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.Clear">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.FilterHint">Filter by name (function or class)...</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.OpenLive">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.CopyName">Name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.SetBaseline">⚑ Set Baseline</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.Diff">Diff vs baseline</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.NewOnly">New/changed only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.ClearBaseline">Clear baseline</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.HideWidgets">Hide UI widgets</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.HideEvents">Hide events/delegates</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.PeriodicOnly">Periodic only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LF.EarliestFirst">Earliest first</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.Start">Start recording which UFunctions the game dispatches through ProcessEvent. Forces the game-thread PE hook to install. After clicking, ALT-TAB to the game and perform ONE action (open the shop, dash, attack), then click Stop — behaviour-based discovery finds the exact function even when its name is unguessable.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.Stop">Stop recording and show the UFunctions that fired, ranked by call count. The action-specific function is usually near the top with a low count (a handful of calls), while per-frame Tick/Update functions have huge counts.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.Refresh">Re-fetch the current fire-count table without stopping — a live peek while recording, or a re-pull after Stop.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.Filter">Substring match against function name OR class name (space = AND, case-insensitive). e.g. "shop" or "open".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.OpenLive">Open this function in Live Walker on a live instance of its class (falls back to Class Struct if the class has no live instance). From there you can invoke it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.CopyName">Copy this function's name to the clipboard.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.SetBaseline">Capture the CURRENT results as a baseline (an idle reference). Do this: Start → stand still a few seconds → Stop → Set Baseline. Then record the ACTION (open shop) and the list flips to a diff — functions that DIDN'T fire while idle ("NEW") rank to the top, so the action's function stops drowning under per-frame Tick/Update noise.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.Diff">Show the "Δ vs base" column and rank new/increased functions first (needs a baseline). Turn off to rank by raw call count again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.NewOnly">Show ONLY functions that are new or fired more than the baseline — the action-specific set. The shop/dash function is almost always in here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.HideWidgets">Hide functions whose owning class is a UI widget (UUserWidget-derived, tagged "UI"). When a shop/menu opens, the widget is CREATED, so all its own methods fire at once and flood the diff — but the widget is the RESULT, not the entry point. Hiding them leaves the persistent opener (on a PlayerController / UI-manager subsystem / interaction component) — the function you can actually call in the open world.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.HideEvents">Hide event handlers and delegate signatures (tagged "Event"/"Deleg" in the Type column — On*/callbacks). These are reactions the engine fires AT the game when something happens, not functions you can call to MAKE it happen. Hiding them leaves imperative callables ("Call"). Note: if this empties the list, the action's real entry point wasn't captured (it opened via a native C++ call the ProcessEvent hook can't see, or the panel hadn't appeared yet — record through until it's fully on screen).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.PeriodicOnly">Show ONLY functions firing at a regular timer-like cadence (tagged "Timer" in the Kind column) — a steady period, low variation, not the per-frame Tick band. The behaviour-based way to find the callback that drives a cooldown / respawn / damage-over-tick: record while standing IDLE for ~15-20 s (the inverse of the action-diff flow), then enable this to leave just the periodic callbacks. The "Period" column shows the measured interval. Caveat: native C++/lambda timers (SetTimer(this, &amp;UClass::Method)) bypass ProcessEvent entirely and never appear here — only UFUNCTION-bound (BP timer event / SetTimerByFunctionName) timers are visible.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LF.EarliestFirst">Sort by call order — the function that fired FIRST during the recording goes to the top (see the "Order" column). An action's entry point runs BEFORE the reactions it triggers (widget creation, On* notifications), so combined with "New/changed only" this floats the true opener to the top instead of ranking by raw call count. Causal, name-independent.</sys:String>

    <!-- Console (UFUNCTION(exec) discovery + invoke) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.Load">Load</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.GameOnly">Game Only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.FilterHint">Filter exec commands by name...</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.ClearFilter">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.CommandHint">type command (e.g. fly, ghost, god)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.Run">Run</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.RunSelected">Run Selected</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.RunRow">Run</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.OpenLive">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.CopyAaScript">AA(B)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.HistoryHeader">Recent invocations</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.Load">Scan every UFunction and surface those marked with UFUNCTION(exec) (FUNC_Exec=0x200). Many games leave these intact in cooked Shipping — they're the cheat surface the developer already curated.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.GameOnly">When OFF (default), engine-side UCheatManager exec commands are included — usually what you want. Flip ON to see only game-specific exec commands.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.ClearFilter">Clear the command-name filter and show the full list again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.Filter">Substring match against command name OR class name (case-insensitive). Empty = show all.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.CommandInput">Type an exec command name and press Run. No-arg commands invoke directly; commands with parameters open the InvokeParamDialog. Leading slash is stripped (/fly == fly).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.RunCommand">Resolve the typed command against the loaded list and invoke. Match is case-insensitive exact funcName; first match wins if multiple classes implement the same name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.RunSelected">Invoke the currently selected row. Same code path as Run on a row.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.RunRow">Invoke this exec command. No-arg commands run directly via pipe; commands with parameters open the InvokeParamDialog.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.OpenLive">Try to open the implementing class in Live Walker. Falls back to Class Struct on CDO-only classes (UCheatManager subclasses often have no live instance until a PlayerController spawns).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.CopyAaScript">Open the Copy AA Script (Baked) dialog for this exec command — useful when you want to bake the invoke into a CE Auto Assembler script for table sharing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.Replay">Re-run this command. Requires the discovery list to still be loaded (re-launch wipes the cache).</sys:String>

    <!-- Console: Debug Camera state-aware helper (visible only when the
         loaded exec list contains ToggleDebugCamera). ToggleDebugCamera is
         a stateful toggle; these controls read the CheatManager's
         DebugCameraControllerRef to show ON/OFF and drive deterministic
         Force On / Force Off instead of blind toggling. -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.DbgCam.Label">Debug Camera</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.DbgCam.ForceOn">Force On</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.DbgCam.ForceOff">Force Off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.DbgCam.Refresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Con.DbgCam.CopyCe">Copy CE Script</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.DbgCam.State">Live Debug Camera state, read from the CheatManager's DebugCameraControllerRef member — the exact field ToggleDebugCamera branches on. ON = a DebugCameraController is active (next plain toggle would turn it OFF). Press ↻ to re-read.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.DbgCam.Refresh">Re-read the live Debug Camera state from the CheatManager instance.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.DbgCam.ForceOn">Ensure Debug Camera is ON: read current state, and fire ToggleDebugCamera only if it is currently OFF. Idempotent — does nothing if already ON.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.DbgCam.ForceOff">Ensure Debug Camera is OFF: read current state, and fire ToggleDebugCamera only if it is currently ON. This is the reliable "turn it off" — plain Run just flips whatever state it's in.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Con.DbgCam.CopyCe">Generate a stateful CE memory-record AA Script (tick = camera ON, untick = camera OFF) — the same robust DLL path as Force On/Off. Self-contained: talks to the DLL mailbox directly, so NO ue5_invoke_helper.lua embed is needed (just UE5Dumper.dll injected). Ships to AOBMaker if connected, else the clipboard.</sys:String>

    <!-- Teleport panel (BugIt-style: marker save/recall + cursor teleport) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PoseHeader">Current Pose</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Refresh">Refresh</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Auto">Auto (0.5s)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Loc">Location (X / Y / Z):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Rot">Rotation (Pitch / Yaw / Roll):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Map">Map:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Pawn">Pawn:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Locate">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateGameEngine">⚙ Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocatePos">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateVel">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateAccel">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateSpeed">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateCamLoc">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateCamFov">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateRot">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LocateCamRot">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Velocity">Velocity (X / Y / Z):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Speed">Speed:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Accel">Acceleration (X / Y / Z):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MarkersHeader">Markers</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Save">Save</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Recall">Recall</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Force">Force</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Clear">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorHeader">Cursor Teleport (2.5D / 45° games)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.ToCursor">Teleport to cursor</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.ZOffset">Z offset:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Channel">Channel:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FallbackCenter">Fall back to screen center</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorHotkey">Global cursor hotkey (keep game focused)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.BugHeader">BugItGo Interop</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CopyBugItGo">Copy as BugItGo</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RunBugItGo">Run</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.BugHint">Paste 'BugItGo X Y Z', 'X Y Z', or a ?BugLoc=(...)?BugRot=(...) string</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LastHeader">Last Position (auto-saved)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LastHint">The dumper records where you were just before every Recall / Force / BugItGo / cursor teleport, so you can undo a jump that went wrong. This slot is managed by the system — you can't save into it, only recall it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RecallLast">Recall last</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.HkHeader">Hotkey Settings</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.HkHint">Global hotkeys for markers (Save / Recall / Recall-last), BugItGo, Debug camera, and the God Mode / Super Jump / Move Speed / Gravity toggles + teleport / cursor actions (fire while the game is focused). Click Set, then press the combo — hold Ctrl/Alt/Shift then a key (e.g. Ctrl+F7), or a single key (e.g. F7). Click Cancel or press Esc to abort. Keyboard only for now (mouse extra-buttons / gamepad not yet supported). Saved automatically. A combo marked ⚠ is held by another app — free it and reconnect, or bind a different one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.ExpHkHeader">Experimental Hotkeys</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.ExpHkHint">Global hotkeys for the experimental features (Keep Foreground ON/OFF, Fly toggle). These are only active while Experimental features is enabled — turning it off unbinds them (and the emergency Keep-Foreground-OFF key). Bind them the same way: click Set, then press the combo.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.HkClear">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CeHeader">CE Export</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.AddActions">Add action records to CE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SaveCt">Save .CT…</sys:String>
    <!-- Global pointers (CE) — DLL-invoke GWorld / GameEngine as CE symbols -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PtrHeader">Global Pointers → Cheat Engine symbols</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PtrHint">Add a CE record that asks the injected UE5Dumper.dll for a live global pointer and publishes it as a registered CE symbol you can reference directly. ENABLE the record → it registers the symbol (UE_GWorld / UE_GameEngine); DISABLE → it unregisters (and frees any buffer). Use the symbol as a pointer base: [UE_GWorld]+offset / [UE_GameEngine]+offset, freeze it, or read it in any record. GWorld is bound to the stable &amp;GWorld slot (auto-follows level changes); GameEngine is a snapshot of the UEngine* (re-tick to refresh). Pushed into Cheat Engine via the AOBMaker plugin; if AOBMaker isn't connected, the CE memory-record XML is copied to the clipboard instead — paste it into CE's address list (right-click → Paste). Needs the DLL injected and a running game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PtrGWorld">Get GWorld</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PtrGameEngine">Get GameEngine</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.PtrGWorld">Add a CE record that resolves the stable &amp;GWorld pointer slot via the DLL and registers it directly as the symbol UE_GWorld, so [UE_GWorld]+offset dereferences the slot to the CURRENT UWorld and chains into it — it auto-follows level transitions (no snapshot). Enable = register, disable = unregister (nothing to free — the slot is a game address). Pushed via AOBMaker, or copied as paste-able CE memory-record XML if AOBMaker isn't connected. Uses the mailbox (CMD_QUERY_PTR), so it works where executeCodeEx can't read export returns.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.PtrGameEngine">Add a CE record that resolves the live UEngine* (GameEngine instance) via the DLL and registers it as the symbol UE_GameEngine, so [UE_GameEngine]+offset chains straight into the engine. Resolved by walking GObjects — no static GEngine slot needed. Enable = register, disable = unregister + free. Pushed via AOBMaker, or copied as paste-able CE memory-record XML if AOBMaker isn't connected. Uses the mailbox (CMD_QUERY_PTR).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Warning">⚠ Single-player only. Online games will rubber-band or may flag teleports.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.BestEffort">ℹ Best-effort, per the design spec (docs/teleport-spec.md): the pawn is resolved by reflection and moved via the engine's own functions where available, with memory-write fallbacks. Works on most games, but some drive the character or the camera from separate internal systems — so on certain titles the character may not move, or it teleports but the camera (POV) doesn't follow.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Refresh">Read the current pawn's world location and control rotation (DLL resolves the local PlayerController → Pawn → RootComponent via reflection).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Auto">Re-read the pose every 0.5s, and the camera POV too (POV is skipped silently on games where it isn't available). Off by default; stops automatically on disconnect.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Locate">Find this pawn (the object whose Current Pose is shown) in the Live Walker and select it — switches to Live Walker, walks the GWorld → … → pawn path, and lands on it. Reads a fresh pose first to get the live pawn address. World-Partition / streaming pawns resolve via the level back-reference (Live Walker shows 'via level'); a just-spawned, unreferenced pawn may be unreachable.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find this pawn via the live UGameEngine graph instead of GWorld. NOTE: a pawn is a world actor and usually lives BELOW GWorld, so this is typically unreachable — use the 🌍 Locate in GWorld button for pawns. Provided only for parity.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocatePos">Locate the position vector in GWorld: walk the GWorld → … → RootComponent path and land directly on the RelativeLocation FVector field (X/Y/Z), so you can build a pointer chain straight to the coordinates. Reads a fresh pose first. (On attached pawns the stored RelativeLocation is parent-relative.)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateVel">Locate the velocity vector in GWorld: walk the GWorld → … → CharacterMovement path and land directly on the Velocity FVector field, for a pointer chain straight to the movement vector. Reads a fresh pose first. Unavailable on pawns with no CharacterMovement (vehicles / custom movement frameworks).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateAccel">Locate the acceleration vector in GWorld: land directly on CharacterMovement.Acceleration (the input acceleration the movement uses). Reads a fresh pose first. Unavailable on pawns with no CharacterMovement.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateSpeed">Speed has no own field — it is the magnitude of Velocity. This lands on the Velocity FVector it is computed from (freeze/inspect the components there). Reads a fresh pose first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateCamLoc">Locate the camera's cached POV Location in GWorld: land on APlayerCameraManager.CameraCachePrivate.POV.Location. Reads a fresh POV first. Unavailable when the cached-POV layout isn't reflected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateCamFov">Locate the camera's cached POV FOV float in GWorld: land on APlayerCameraManager.CameraCachePrivate.POV.FOV. Reads a fresh POV first. Unavailable when the cached-POV layout isn't reflected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateRot">Locate the rotation in GWorld: land directly on Controller.ControlRotation (the FRotator Pitch/Yaw/Roll that drives the view/aim). Reads a fresh pose first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LocateCamRot">Locate the camera's cached POV Rotation in GWorld: land on APlayerCameraManager.CameraCachePrivate.POV.Rotation. Reads a fresh POV first. Unavailable when the cached-POV layout isn't reflected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Velocity">Live velocity (cm/s) and acceleration (cm/s²) read straight from the pawn's CharacterMovement (no invoke). Enable Auto to watch them change while moving. Shown as — with a note on pawns that have no CharacterMovement (vehicles / custom movement frameworks).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Save">Save the current pose into this marker slot. Markers live in the DLL — they survive a UI restart as long as the game process lives.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Recall">Teleport back to this marker (invokes K2_SetActorLocation with bTeleport=true, then restores rotation and zeroes velocity). Refused if you've changed maps — use Force.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Force">Recall even if the marker was saved on a different map. May land you in an unloaded area — use with care.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Clear">Forget this marker.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.HkClear">Clear this row's hotkey binding.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.ToCursor">Teleport the pawn to the world position under the mouse cursor (or screen center if no cursor). Uses K2_TeleportTo so the pawn lands beside geometry, not inside it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.ZOffset">Height added above the hit point so the capsule doesn't spawn intersecting the ground (default 100).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.Channel">ETraceTypeQuery byte. 0 = Visibility (default engine mapping). Games can remap channels — change this if cursor hits land on the wrong surface.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FallbackCenter">When the game has no cursor (HD-2D-style), trace from the screen center instead.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CursorHotkey">Bind a system-wide hotkey for cursor teleport so you can keep the game focused (the cursor stays in the game). Auto-picks the first free combo: Ctrl+F8→F5, then Alt+F8→F5. The chosen combo shows beside this box.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CopyBugItGo">Read the current pose, copy a 'BugItGo X Y Z' string to the clipboard, and paste it into the Run field so you can fire BugItGo immediately. (BugIt itself can't return data to the dumper — it writes to the game's own log/clipboard — so this reads the pose directly.)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.RunBugItGo">Parse the pasted string and teleport there. Works without any CheatManager — feeds X/Y/Z into the same recall path as markers.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.RecallLast">Undo the most recent teleport: jump back to the pose auto-saved just before your last Recall / Force / BugItGo / cursor teleport. One-way — recalling it doesn't change the saved spot, so you can press it repeatedly. The system writes this slot; you can't save into it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.HkSet">Click, then press the key combo you want for this action. Hold modifiers (Ctrl/Alt/Shift) then a key, or press a single key. Esc cancels. The combo is registered system-wide so it works while the game is focused.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.AddActions">Ship the 17 momentary teleport records (Save 1-3, Recall 1-3, Recall last, BugIt, BugItGo, Cursor, Get POV, Get current coords, TP facing direction, TP to coordinates, Cursor ON, Cursor OFF, Clear all) PLUS 3 movement on/off toggles (Move Speed, Gravity, Super Jump — baked at the current slider %, tick = apply, untick = OFF) into the open Cheat Engine table via AOBMaker. Teleport records fire once and auto-untick; movement records stay on while ticked (the DLL holds the value). The directional/coordinate/movement records bake the current field values. Requires the AOBMaker plugin connected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SaveCt">Backup: save a Cheat Engine .CT file with the 17 teleport records + 3 movement on/off toggles (Move Speed / Gravity / Super Jump baked at the current slider %), for when AOBMaker isn't connected. Import via CE File → Open.</sys:String>
    <!-- Teleport: Debug Camera (free-look) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcHeader">Debug Camera (free-look)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcHint">Detach the view into a free-fly debug camera (UCheatManager::ToggleDebugCamera). Turning it back OFF is handled even on Shipping builds that stripped the disable path — the DLL switches the local player's controller back. Needs a live PlayerController, so enter gameplay first. Available only on games that kept ToggleDebugCamera.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcOn">Force ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcOff">Force OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.DcRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.DcOn">Deterministically enable the debug camera (reads state first, only toggles if needed). Idempotent — pressing again when it's already ON does nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.DcOff">Deterministically disable the debug camera and return control to the player. On builds that stripped the disable path, the DLL swaps the local player's controller back to the original PlayerController.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.DcRefresh">Re-read the live debug-camera state (1=ON / 0=OFF / unknown if no live CheatManager).</sys:String>

    <!-- God Mode (Solitar — force AActor.bCanBeDamaged) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmHeader">God Mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmHint">Forces the player pawn's AActor.bCanBeDamaged to FALSE, so damage routed through the standard engine pipeline (ApplyDamage) is dropped. Re-asserted on a timer while ON, so it survives respawns. Needs a live pawn — enter gameplay first. Games with custom health systems may bypass it: then freeze the health value in Value Search, or find the game's own immortal flag in Property Search. Single-player only — online games replicate this flag server-side.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmOn">Force ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmOff">Force OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GmCopyScript">Add to CE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GmOn">Turn God Mode ON (clear bCanBeDamaged). Idempotent; the DLL re-asserts the bit every ~300 ms so it survives respawns.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GmOff">Turn God Mode OFF (restore bCanBeDamaged) and stop the re-assert worker.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GmRefresh">Re-read the live God Mode state (1=immune / 0=can be damaged / unknown if no pawn).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GmCopyScript">Add a self-contained Cheat Engine AA record (tick = ON, untick = OFF) that drives God Mode via the DLL mailbox. Pushed straight into CE via AOBMaker, or copied as paste-able CE memory-record XML if AOBMaker isn't connected (right-click the address list → Paste). Works offline — no pipe / UI connection needed.</sys:String>

    <!-- Player Stealth meter (Solide — auto-find + hold a stealth/visibility float at 0) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StHeader">Stealth Meter</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StHint">Auto-finds a numeric "visibility / noise / detection / awareness" meter on the player pawn (and its owned components) and holds it at 0 so detection that reads that meter never fills. There is NO universal detection flag in Unreal — this only works when the game exposes a reflected stealth/visibility field. Click Detect, verify the field it locked onto, then Hold @0. If nothing is found, search Property Search (e.g. 'visib', 'detect', 'noise') and Force a numeric field to 0. Single-player only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StDetect">Detect meter</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StHold">Hold @0</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.StReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.StDetect">Resolve the player pawn + its owned components and keyword-score reflected numeric fields for a stealth/visibility/detection meter. Read-only — loads the top candidate into the readout for you to verify before holding it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.StHold">Force the detected meter to 0 on every live instance of its class and hold it there (re-asserted on a timer). "N held" shows how many instances are held.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.StReset">Release the hold — restore the captured base value and stop holding the meter.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgHeader">Keep Foreground</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgHint">Force the game to always behave as the foreground app so it doesn't idle/pause its game thread when unfocused (t.IdleWhenNotForeground). Needed for POV / UFunction invokes on games that halt when backgrounded — e.g. Persona 3 Reload. Off by default.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgOn">Force ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgOff">Force OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FgOn">Turn the foreground lock ON — the DLL hooks GetForegroundWindow so the game always thinks it is focused. Keeps the game thread ticking (invokes / POV) while this tool or Cheat Engine holds the foreground.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FgOff">Turn the foreground lock OFF — restore normal focus behaviour (the game may idle/pause when unfocused).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FgRefresh">Re-read the live foreground-lock state (ON / OFF).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FgCopyScript">Add to CE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FgCopyScript">Add a self-contained Cheat Engine AA record (tick = ON, untick = OFF) that drives Keep Foreground via the DLL mailbox. Pushed straight into CE via AOBMaker, or copied as paste-able CE memory-record XML if AOBMaker isn't connected (right-click the address list → Paste). Works offline — no pipe / UI connection needed.</sys:String>

    <!-- Standalone trainer export (no-DLL CE Lua .CT via AOBMaker) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TrainerHeader">Standalone Trainer (no DLL)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TrainerExport">Export to CE (this game/version)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.TrainerExport">Bake this game's GWorld chain + offsets into a self-contained CE Lua trainer (Move Speed / Gravity / Super Jump / GodMode / coordinate TP) and push it straight into Cheat Engine via the AOBMaker plugin. Runs WITHOUT the DLL afterwards. Valid only for this exact game build — regenerate after a patch. Requires the AOBMaker plugin.</sys:String>

    <!-- Move Speed multiplier (Laufen — force UCharacterMovementComponent.MaxWalkSpeed) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsHeader">Move Speed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsHint">Scales the player pawn's UCharacterMovementComponent.MaxWalkSpeed by the slider multiplier (10%–1000%, log scale, 100% = base). The DLL captures the original value on first Apply and re-asserts the target every ~250 ms so it survives respawns and per-tick recomputation. Only normal walk speed is engine-standard — sprint / dash are game-specific (find them in Property Search). Needs a live pawn with a CharacterMovement (not vehicles / custom movement). Single-player only — online games replicate movement server-side.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsApply">Apply</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.MsLocate">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.MsApply">Force MaxWalkSpeed to base × the slider multiplier and hold it (re-asserted on a timer so a sprint/ability system can't revert it).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.MsReset">Restore MaxWalkSpeed to its captured base value and stop holding it. Resets the slider to 100%.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.MsRefresh">Re-read the live MaxWalkSpeed and override state from the current pawn's CharacterMovement.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.MsLocate">Find the MaxWalkSpeed float in GWorld and select it in the Live Walker — to freeze or inspect it directly.</sys:String>

    <!-- Time dilation (Hemmung) — global slow-mo / freeze / speed-up -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdHeader">Time Dilation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdSubtitle">slow-mo / bullet time / freeze / fast-forward</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdHint">Slows, freezes, or speeds up game time by forcing a reflected dilation float. The two levers are independent and held together: "Whole world" writes AWorldSettings.TimeDilation (everything — enemies, projectiles, physics); "Player pawn" writes the pawn's AActor.CustomTimeDilation. The pawn's effective rate is world × pawn, so World 0.5× + Player 2× = the player at normal speed in a half-speed world (bullet time). The DLL captures each natural value on first Apply and re-asserts it every ~250 ms, so a slow-mo ability or Sequencer time-track can't revert it. 1× = normal, 0.5× = half speed, 0 = frozen (a literal 0 can destabilise physics on some games — use a small value like 0.05×). A paused world can't be stepped this way. Single-player only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdWorldHeader">Whole world (AWorldSettings.TimeDilation)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdPawnHeader">Player pawn (AActor.CustomTimeDilation)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdApply">Apply</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdRefreshBoth">↻ Refresh both</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdFreeze">Freeze</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdQuarter">¼×</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdHalf">½×</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdNormal">1×</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.TdDouble">2×</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.TdApply">Hold this lever at the slider's absolute dilation and re-assert it on a timer so the game can't revert it. World and player are independent — applying one doesn't disturb the other.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.TdReset">Restore this lever to its captured natural value and stop holding it. Resets its slider to 1×; the other lever is untouched.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.TdRefresh">Re-read both levers' live dilation values and override state.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.TdEffective">The player's real speed is world × pawn — UE multiplies the global world dilation into the pawn's CustomTimeDilation, so "Whole world" slows the player too. For a slow world but a normal-speed player (bullet time), set Player = 1 ÷ World (e.g. World 0.5× → Player 2×).</sys:String>

    <!-- Gravity multiplier (Laufen — force UCharacterMovementComponent.GravityScale) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrHeader">Gravity</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrHint">Scales the player pawn's UCharacterMovementComponent.GravityScale by the slider (10%–1000%, log scale, 100% = base). Below 100% = floaty / moon-jump; above = heavy. Held against per-tick overwrites by the re-assert worker. Per-pawn only — it does not change world gravity for other actors. Extreme values can clip you through floors or break fall damage. Arbitrary gravity direction (UE5.4+) is not yet exposed. Needs a live pawn with a CharacterMovement. Single-player only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrApply">Apply</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GrLocate">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GrApply">Force GravityScale to base × the slider multiplier and hold it (re-asserted on a timer).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GrReset">Restore GravityScale to its captured base value. Resets the slider to 100%.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GrRefresh">Re-read the live GravityScale and override state from the current pawn's CharacterMovement.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GrLocate">Find the GravityScale float in GWorld and select it in the Live Walker.</sys:String>

    <!-- Super Jump toggle (Laufen — force UCharacterMovementComponent.JumpZVelocity) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjHeader">Super Jump</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjHint">Persistent high-jump toggle: forces UCharacterMovementComponent.JumpZVelocity and re-asserts it. The slider is jump HEIGHT (10%–3000%, 100% = base); apex height grows with the square of launch velocity, so the applied velocity multiplier is √(height) — e.g. 400% height = ×2 velocity. Force ON holds it; Force OFF restores the base, Reset also snaps the slider to 100%. There is no engine "jump height" property, so this works by scaling the launch impulse. Needs a live pawn with a CharacterMovement. Single-player only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjOn">Force ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjOff">Force OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjLocate">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SjHotkeyHint">Bind a global hotkey to "Super Jump toggle" in the Marker Hotkeys section above to toggle it in-game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SjOn">Turn Super Jump ON — set JumpZVelocity to base × √(slider height %) and hold it (survives respawns).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SjOff">Turn Super Jump OFF — restore JumpZVelocity to its captured base.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SjReset">Turn Super Jump OFF and snap the height slider back to 100%.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SjRefresh">Re-read the live JumpZVelocity and override state from the current pawn's CharacterMovement.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SjLocate">Find the JumpZVelocity float in GWorld and select it in the Live Walker.</sys:String>

    <!-- Fly (Dunste — no-gravity keyboard-driven 3D flight) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyHeader">Fly (no-gravity 3D flight)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyHint">No-gravity free flight: forces the pawn's CharacterMovement into MOVE_Flying and steers it with the keyboard. Input is read inside the game, so keep the GAME window focused. Control is VIEW-relative: forward/back/strafe fly where the camera looks (you turn with the mouse/gamepad as usual), and the up/down keys move straight up/down. Pick a keyboard preset and a speed below, then Fly ON / OFF (or bind the "Fly toggle" global hotkey). Default keeps collision (you won't pass through walls); tick Noclip to disable the character's collision and fly through geometry. Needs a live pawn with a CharacterMovement. Single-player only; a few games with an authoritative movement system (e.g. FF7 Rebirth) override the velocity and won't move.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyPreset">Keys:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyNoclip">Noclip (fly through walls)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FlyNoclip">OFF = collision kept (the flying sweep blocks on walls). ON = disable the character's collision (SetActorEnableCollision) so flight passes through geometry. Movement is the same velocity-drive either way; only collision changes. Collision is restored when you turn Noclip or Fly off.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyOn">Fly ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyOff">Fly OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.FlyHotkeyHint">Bind a global hotkey to "Fly toggle" in the Hotkey Settings section above to toggle Fly in-game without alt-tabbing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FlyPreset">Which keys drive flight: WASD (+Q/E turn, Z/C up-down), the numeric keypad, or the arrow keys (+Ins/Del turn, PgUp/PgDn up-down).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FlyOn">Turn Fly ON — force MOVE_Flying and start reading the keyboard (held against the game re-asserting its own movement mode).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FlyOff">Turn Fly OFF — restore the pawn's captured MovementMode and stop.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.FlyRefresh">Re-read the live fly state and MovementMode from the current pawn's CharacterMovement.</sys:String>

    <!-- See-through occluders (Schlacht) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughHeader">See-through (occluders)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughHint">Hide the object blocking your view: each tick traces a line from the camera along the view direction and, if the NEAREST thing in the way is world geometry (a wall/pillar), makes it invisible. Enemies, NPCs and your own character are never hidden (only non-Pawn/Character actors). Stage 1 hides one nearest occluder at a time; it is un-hidden as you look around and everything is restored when you turn it off. Needs a live pawn + camera. Single-player only; best-effort. NOTE: works on games where an object's collision and visible mesh are the same actor (most games) — on games that split them into separate collision-proxy actors (e.g. FF7 Rebirth's CollisionAssetActor) the ray hits the invisible collision proxy, so hiding it has no visible effect. TIP: turning it OFF needs the game running — clicking here backgrounds the game, and a game that pauses when it loses focus cannot un-hide right then. It is restored automatically the moment you click back into the game, and the Keep Foreground card above prevents the pause entirely.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughOn">See-through ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughOff">See-through OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughAddCe">Add to CE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughPierce">Pierce depth:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SeeThroughPierce">How many nearest objects to make invisible along the view ray. 1 = just the nearest object (e.g. a painting on a wall); 2 = it plus the wall behind it; and so on. Enemies / NPCs / your character on the ray are skipped (kept visible) and do not count. Changing this while See-through is ON applies immediately.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.SeeThroughHotkeyHint">Bind a global hotkey to "See-through toggle" in the Experimental Hotkeys section above to toggle it in-game without alt-tabbing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SeeThroughOn">Turn See-through ON — start the worker that traces the view direction and hides the nearest non-Pawn occluder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SeeThroughOff">Turn See-through OFF — un-hide any hidden object and stop the worker.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SeeThroughRefresh">Re-read the live see-through state (active / whether it has a target / how many objects are hidden).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.SeeThroughAddCe">Push a self-contained CE toggle script (tick = ON / untick = OFF) straight into Cheat Engine via AOBMaker. It drives the DLL's See-through through the mailbox, so no pipe connection is needed once added. Disabled when the AOBMaker CE plugin isn't detected.</sys:String>

    <!-- Gravity Direction (Laufen — UE5.4+ UCharacterMovementComponent.GravityDirection) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdHeader">Gravity Direction (UE5.4+)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdState">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdHint">Tilt gravity to any direction by setting the per-axis components (each −100%…+100%, i.e. −1.0…+1.0). This is DIRECTION only — the DLL normalizes to a unit vector, so a single-axis setting like (0,0,−62%) collapses to (0,0,−1): it only re-aims gravity, it does NOT change strength (use the Gravity card for strength). To tilt you must mix axes, e.g. X=+100%, Z=−100% → diagonal. Default (0,0,−100%) = straight down; X=+100%, Z=0 makes gravity pull along +X (walk on a wall). Apply holds the direction; Reset restores the game's captured default and snaps the sliders back to down. Needs UE5.4+ with a reflected GravityDirection (pre-5.4 games show Unavailable). Single-player only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdApply">Apply</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdReset">Reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdLocate">🌍 Locate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GdHotkeyHint">Bind a global hotkey to "Gravity Dir toggle" in the Hotkey Settings section to toggle it in-game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GdApply">Force GravityDirection to the (X,Y,Z) sliders (normalized) and hold it (re-asserted on a timer). All-zero = off.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GdReset">Restore GravityDirection to the captured game default and snap the sliders to straight down (0,0,−100%).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GdRefresh">Re-read the live GravityDirection and override state from the current pawn's CharacterMovement (UE5.4+).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GdLocate">Find the GravityDirection FVector in GWorld and select it in the Live Walker.</sys:String>

    <!-- Teleport: Camera POV (read-only) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PovHeader">Camera POV (read-only)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.GetPov">Get POV</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.Fov">FOV:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.PovHint">The on-screen camera (APlayerCameraManager) — its world location, rotation and field-of-view, read via the engine's GetCameraLocation / GetCameraRotation / GetFOVAngle. This is DISTINCT from the pawn pose above: on games that drive the camera independently of the possessed pawn (HD-2D / fixed-view) the two diverge. Read-only — there is no universal Set POV (the view is recomputed every frame); to actually move the camera, use the Debug Camera buttons below.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.GetPov">Read the current on-screen camera location, rotation and FOV (invokes APlayerCameraManager getters on the game thread). Try it during gameplay — in menus/loading the game thread is idle and it can't read. Compare the location to the pawn's: a large delta that barely changes after a teleport means the camera is independent of the pawn.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.PovSource">How the POV was read: "invoke" = the camera-manager getters; "raw" = a direct read of the cached POV, used as a fallback on games where the getters exist but return nothing (e.g. TQ2 / Octopath).</sys:String>

    <!-- Teleport: directional / coordinate / cursor force -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RelHeader">Teleport in Facing Direction</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RelGo">Teleport forward</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RelDist">Distance (uu):</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.RelHoriz">Horizontal only (keep Z)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.RelGo">Step the pawn along the direction it is facing by the distance below (negative distance = backward). The facing comes from the actor's forward vector (GetActorForwardVector), falling back to the control rotation. Undo with Recall last.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.RelDist">How far to move along the facing direction, in unreal units (1 uu ≈ 1 cm). Negative values step backward.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.RelHoriz">On: move on the ground plane (yaw only, keep your current height) — the natural "walk toward that compass bearing". Off: follow the full view direction including pitch, so looking up/down moves you up/down (fly / noclip feel).</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordHeader">Teleport to Coordinates (force)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordXyz">X / Y / Z:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordPyr">Pitch / Yaw / Roll:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordGo">Teleport to coordinates</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordFill">Fill from current pos</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CoordSetRot">Also set rotation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CoordGo">Force-teleport the pawn to the exact world X/Y/Z below. No map check, so you can jump anywhere on the current level. Undo with Recall last.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CoordFill">Read the current pose and fill the X/Y/Z (and Pitch/Yaw/Roll) boxes with it — a quick way to nudge from where you are.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CoordSetRot">When ticked, also restore the Pitch/Yaw/Roll below after the jump. Leave off to keep your current facing.</sys:String>

    <!-- Coordinate Library (P1) — docs/teleport-coord-library-spec.md -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibHint">An unlimited, labelled list of positions saved per game. Separate from the 3 marker slots above: those live in the DLL and are hotkey-driven, this one is a curated list that persists across game patches.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibSaveCurrent">＋ Save current pos</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibAddFields">＋ From fields</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibDuplicate">Duplicate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibDelete">Delete</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear">Clear all</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.ConfirmTitle">Clear the whole coordinate library?</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.ConfirmMessage">This deletes all {0} entries for this game and removes the library file.

A .preclear.bak copy is written first, so this can be recovered by hand from the app data folder.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.ConfirmYes">Clear all</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.Empty">The library is already empty.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.Result">Cleared {0} entries.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibClear.ResultBackedUp">Cleared {0} entries. Backed up to {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibFilter">Filter:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibFilterPlaceholder">space = AND (label, group, map)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibGroup">Group:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibCurrentMapOnly">Current map only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibGo">Teleport</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibForceGo">Force teleport</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibToFields">→ Fields</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibEditLabel">Label:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibEditGroup">Group:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibApplyEdit">Apply</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibUpdateCoords">Update coords from current</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibSaveCurrent">Read the live pose and add it to the library, labelled after the current map.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibAddFields">Add whatever is currently typed in the "Teleport to Coordinates" boxes above as a new library entry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibGo">Teleport to the selected entry. Refused when the entry was saved on a different map — the tool cannot LOAD another map, it can only move you within the one the game has open.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibForceGo">Teleport ignoring the map check. Only useful if the map name changed but the level is the same; jumping to another map's coordinates puts you outside the world.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibCurrentMapOnly">Hide entries saved on other maps. On by default — coordinates from different maps are often numerically close, so mixing them is how you end up under the floor.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibUpdateCoords">Overwrite the selected entry's coordinates (and map) with the live pose, keeping its label and group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibClear">Delete every entry for this game, and its file. Asks first, and writes a .preclear.bak you can restore by hand.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibExportCsv">Export CSV</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibExportTemplate">Sample CSV</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportCsv">Import CSV…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportReplace">Replace (not merge)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportApply">Apply import</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportCancel">Cancel import</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibExportCsv">Write the WHOLE library (not just the filtered rows) as UTF-8 CSV with a BOM, so Excel opens non-ASCII labels correctly.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibExportTemplate">Write a small sample CSV — a header plus three example rows — to fill in by hand.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibImportCsv">Read a CSV and show what it WOULD change. Nothing is written until you press Apply. Columns are matched by header name, so extra or reordered columns are fine.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibImportReplace">On: the file becomes the whole library and anything missing from it is deleted. Off (default): only add new entries and update matching ones.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibExportLua">Push to CE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibSaveLua">Save .lua</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibExportLua">Emit the whole library as a Cheat Engine AA script with its own filterable picker. Delivery is via the AOBMaker CE plugin; without it the script is copied to the clipboard as paste-able CE XML instead. The generated script needs UE5Dumper.dll injected — it teleports through the DLL mailbox.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibSaveLua">Write the same AA script to a .lua file instead of pushing it into Cheat Engine.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportLua">Parse pasted script</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibImportLuaFile">Import .lua…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibPastePlaceholder">Paste a previously generated coordinate script here (the whole thing — only the @UE5CD:COORDS block is read)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibImportLua">Read the coordinates back out of a script you exported earlier and hand-edited. Only the fenced @UE5CD:COORDS block is parsed; the rest of the script is ignored and never executed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibImportLuaFile">Same, but read the script from a .lua file.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibExportLuaNoDll">Push to CE (no DLL)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibExportLuaNoDll">Same picker, but the generated script teleports by raw memory write, so UE5Dumper.dll does NOT have to be injected. "No DLL" refers to that only. REQUIRES the AOBMaker CE plugin and is disabled without it — unlike the button on its left there is deliberately no clipboard fallback, because the script calls helpers defined by the Standalone Trainer's Setup record, which only an AOBMaker push can deliver. Lands in the "UE5CEDumper (no-DLL trainer)" group; enable "UE5 Trainer: Setup" before using it. Weaker than the DLL path: no map guard (the map name is only readable through the DLL), and the offsets go stale when the game is patched.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.LibZTol">Z tolerance:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.LibZTol">Extra height (uu) added to Z on arrival, so landing exactly on the floor the position was captured on does not drop you through it. 0 = off. Applied at teleport time only — the saved entry keeps its exact captured Z — and baked into the Lua export so the generated picker behaves the same. Not part of the CSV.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorForceHeader">Force Mouse Cursor</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorForceHint">Force the OS mouse cursor visible or hidden by writing the PlayerController's bShowMouseCursor. Handy when a game hides the cursor but you want Cursor Teleport. One-shot: a game that re-sets the flag every frame may revert it, and a captured input mode can still hide the cursor.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorStateLabel">State:</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorOn">Force ON</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorOff">Force OFF</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.TP.CursorRefresh">↻</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CursorOn">Show the mouse cursor (sets bShowMouseCursor = true on the local PlayerController).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CursorOff">Hide the mouse cursor (sets bShowMouseCursor = false).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.TP.CursorRefresh">Re-read the live bShowMouseCursor state (1=ON / 0=OFF / unknown if no live PlayerController).</sys:String>

    <!-- Interesting Properties Finder (B' / Unusual Location) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.Load">Load</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.GameOnly">Game Only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.FilterHint">Filter by name (property or class)...</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.UnusualOnly">⚠ Unusual Only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.ShowAll">Show All (incl. low-score)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.ClearFilters">Clear</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.OpenInst">inst</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.OpenLive">Live</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.LocateGWorld">🌍</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.LocateGameEngine">⚙</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.CopyName">Name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.Load">Fan-out search across the seed keyword list (HP/Damage/Gold/Speed/etc.) and score the results client-side. Highlights properties in non-canonical locations (LocalPlayer / HUD / GameViewportClient) — the kind of cheat-relevant fields a CE user would miss looking only in Character/Pawn.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.GameOnly">Skip /Script/Engine, /Script/CoreUObject, etc. Recommended on — game-specific properties are the cheat-table targets.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.Filter">Substring match against property name OR class name (case-insensitive). e.g. "Health" or "PlayerState".</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.ClearFilters">Clear the name filter, category, and the Unusual-only / Show-all toggles (reset to defaults).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.OpenInst">Open this row's class in the Instance Finder tab — pre-fills the class name and runs the search to list its live instances.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.Category">Filter by category. Stats / Combat / Resources / Movement / Utility / Other.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.UnusualOnly">Show only properties whose owning class is in the Unusual Location list (LocalPlayer / GameViewportClient / HUD / CheatManager) — properties developers placed outside the conventional Character/Pawn/PlayerState containers.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.ShowAll">When off, hide rows below the InterestingThreshold (currently 4). When on, show every property regardless of score.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.OpenLive">Open this property's owning class in Live Walker (live instance lookup), or fall back to Class Struct if no live instance exists.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find a live instance of this property's class, then show the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it). Disabled until GWorld is resolved.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find a live instance of this property's class, then show the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it). Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.CopyName">Copy this property's name to the clipboard.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.IP.GenerateCT">📦 Generate CT</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.IP.GenerateCT">Generate one .CT file containing freeze entries for every selected row. Use Ctrl/Shift+click in the grid to multi-select first. Each entry uses ue5_freeze_helper.lua — embed it in the resulting CT via Tools → Inject Freeze Helper, or open the CT in CE and edit CFG.value before enabling. Struct / array / non-scalar rows are skipped.</sys:String>

    <!-- Proxy DLL / Deferred Scan -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Toolbar.StartScan">Start Scan</sys:String>

    <!-- Errors -->

    <!-- Tooltips: MainWindow toolbar -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Connect">Connect to injected DLL via named pipe</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Disconnect">Disconnect from DLL pipe server</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.StartScan">Trigger deferred scan (proxy DLL mode)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.AddressFormat">Address display format (absolute / module-relative)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.CollapsePointers">Collapse pointer chain nodes in CE XML export</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ArrayLimit">Max array elements to read inline (2^N)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.DropDownLimit">Max CE DropDownList entries (2^N)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.StringLength">CE String field display length in characters (2^N; 16-4096). Applies to Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field. A generous value never truncates a shorter live string (CE stops at the null terminator) — it reserves room for strings that grow between saves.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Fabricate">Copy CE Field only: 0 = Off. When set (2^N = 2..4096), the SELECTED top-level TArray is padded to this many element rows — null pointers and indices beyond the live Num are fabricated using a resolved element's field layout, so the CE table already has slots for items a later save/ship will hold. Only the selected array is padded, never nested arrays inside its elements. TArray only (Map/Set are sparse and never fabricated). Rows past the current Num read past-the-end memory (harmless) until the game grows the array. Values above 256 (use at your own risk) can produce very large tables that slow Cheat Engine or hit the export entry cap.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PreviewLimit">Struct sub-fields shown in Value column (0 = no preview)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.CsxDepth">Pointer drill-down depth (0 = flat). Applies to both CSX export and CE XML / CE Field copy. 0-4 normal; 5-6 deep — output size grows exponentially per level (depth 6 on UWorld can produce multi-MB CE XML).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Toolbar.Options">Export-shape settings: pointer-node collapse, array/dropdown/preview limits, drill depth. Set once per session.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Export">Export object data in various formats</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools">Tools that don't depend on a live DLL connection (helper file export/inject, etc.)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.InjectCeBootstrap">Route ③ of 4 — a one-off record for the table you have open. If you work in Cheat Engine a lot, prefer Tools ▸ Install CE autorun Helper instead: that gives EVERY table an ue5_inject() permanently. (① is deploying a proxy DLL from the Proxy Deploy tab; ② is injecting into a running game; ④ is the standalone dist\UE5CEDumper.CT.) Adds an [ENABLE]/[DISABLE] record that injects UE5Dumper.dll and starts the pipe server, straight into the CE table you already have open — so you never have to load the standalone .CT first (Cheat Engine holds one table at a time). Tick the record to inject; it polls the DLL for readiness instead of sleeping a fixed 15s, and reports a real error on failure. The standalone .CT is still shipped for developer use. Needs the AOBMaker CE Plugin; without it the record is copied to the clipboard as CE XML to paste in.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.InstallCeAutorun">Writes ue5_autorun.lua into &lt;CheatEngine&gt;\autorun\, which CE runs at start-up — so ue5_inject() and ue5_shutdown() then exist in EVERY table, permanently, and a "UE5CEDumper: Inject DLL" entry appears in CE's main menu. This is the only route that needs neither the standalone .CT nor the AOBMaker plugin, so it is the best option when you work in Cheat Engine a lot. Installs straight into a running Cheat Engine when one is found; otherwise you choose where to save. Takes effect the NEXT time Cheat Engine starts. Re-run this after moving UE5DumpUI — the DLL path is baked into the file.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.InjectCeHelperLua">One-click: send ue5_invoke_helper.lua straight into the currently open CE table via the AOBMaker plugin. Replaces existing copy if present. Skips the manual save-to-disk + Table -&gt; Add File... dance. Requires the AOBMaker CE Plugin to be loaded (open Cheat Engine first); falls back gracefully if unavailable.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.ExportCeHelperLua">Save ue5_invoke_helper.lua to a file you can drop next to your .CT and add via Cheat Engine's Table -&gt; Add File... menu. Required by every "Copy AA Script (Baked)" output -- one-time setup per cheat table.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.InjectFreezeHelperLua">One-click: send ue5_freeze_helper.lua into the currently open CE table via the AOBMaker plugin. Required once per .CT by every Property Search "Freeze" script. Coexists with ue5_invoke_helper.lua.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Tools.ExportFreezeHelperLua">Save ue5_freeze_helper.lua to a file you can drop next to your .CT and add via Cheat Engine's Table -&gt; Add File... menu. Manual fallback when AOBMaker plugin is unavailable.</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Object Tree -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.Reload">Reload all objects from game process</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.Cancel">Cancel current loading operation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.Search">Search objects by name or class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.SearchField">Type a keyword and press Enter or the magnifier to search every object in the game server-side. Multiple space-separated terms are ANDed, each matching the object name OR class name. Respects the "Instances only" toggle. Results are capped (you'll be told if more exist — narrow, or Reload + use the bottom filter for the whole pool uncapped). Leave it empty and press the magnifier (or Reload) to list all objects again. Recent searches are suggested as you type.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.ClassFilter">Filter by class type</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.Collapse">Collapse the Object Tree to give the right-hand panels the full window width</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.Expand">Show the Object Tree</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.TextFilter">Filter the loaded objects by text. Separate multiple terms with spaces — every term must match (AND), each against Name, Class, or Address. Example: "BP_ char" keeps objects matching both BP_ and char. Leave the Search box empty + press the magnifier (or Reload) to list all again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ObjectTree.InstancesOnly">Hide the reflection/type layer (UClass, UFunction, UScriptStruct, UEnum, UPackage, and UE4 property descriptors) so only live gameplay instances remain. Applies to BOTH the top Search (server-side) and the bottom text filter, over the ENTIRE loaded pool — not just the visible page. Reload + the bottom filter scans the whole pool uncapped; the top Search is faster but caps its results.</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Instance Finder -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.ClassName">Class name to search for (substring by default, e.g. "Character"). Tick Exact match for a whole-name match. Combine with the Object name box (AND), or leave this empty to search by object name alone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.AddressField">An address to reverse-look-up — hex (0x7FF…) or module.exe+offset. Finds the UObject that contains it (or the nearest one / the container it lives in).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.Search">Search for instances by class name and/or object name (at least one is required)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.ObjectName">Optional: also require the object's NAME to contain this text (case-insensitive). Combined with the class name as AND. Leave the class box empty to search by object name alone. Filtering runs in the game (across every object) so you won't miss matches hidden behind unrelated objects — but the result list is still capped; if you see "⚠ capped" in the status, narrow the search.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.ExactMatch">Match class name exactly (no substring). Applies to the class box only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.NewestFirst">Scan from the newest (highest InternalIndex) end → catches just-spawned instances (e.g. an enemy that just appeared) and avoids the result cap truncating the newest. Off = oldest first (class-default object / template / earliest instances — good for finding a Blueprint's defaults). Tip: click the Index column to re-sort either way.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.MaxResults">Maximum rows returned (100–50000). The game-side scan is always exhaustive; this only caps the returned list. Raise it if you see "⚠ capped". Larger values are slower to transfer + filter.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.Keyword">Temporary filter over the rows already returned — whitespace-separated terms are ANDed across Name / Class / Address (e.g. "BP_ char"). Instant + client-side: it does NOT reach past the result cap. To recover an instance hidden past the cap, exclude noise classes in the class-filter (⚑) — that re-runs the scan in the game — or raise Max.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.Lookup">Lookup UObject at the specified address</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.CopyAddr">Copy instance address to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.OpenWalker">Open this instance in Live Walker</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.LocateGWorld">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this instance and show it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.LocateGWorldContainer">Locate in GWorld: find the shortest pointer path from GWorld down to this container's owning object, then drill into the element so you land next to the value</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.LocateGameEngine">Locate in GameEngine: find the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this instance and show it in Live Walker (stops at the parent that points to it). Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.LocateGameEngineContainer">Locate in GameEngine: find the shortest pointer path from the live UGameEngine down to this container's owning object, then drill into the element so you land next to the value. Reaches engine-layer objects the GWorld graph can't — for most world actors, use Locate in GWorld.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.ExportCE">Export instance as CE XML pointer chain</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.CopyFieldAddr">Copy field absolute address</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.AA">Generate CE Auto Assembler script</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.CopyName">Copy the object name to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.InstanceFinder.FindFunc">Find UFunctions that take this instance's class as a parameter or return value (reflection scan — includes native functions, unlike the per-field bytecode xref)</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Property Search -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.Query">Property name to search for (substring, case-insensitive), e.g. Health, Mana, Gold. Combine with the Type filter to narrow further. Press Enter or Search to run.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.Search">Search across all classes. Either Name or Type must be set; comma-separate multiple types.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.TypeFilter">Restrict by FProperty type. Type a few characters to filter the suggestion dropdown (e.g. 'opt' → OptionalProperty). Comma-separated for OR.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.ResultFilter">Filter the current result list locally (case-insensitive substring across Class / Property / Type / Super / Preview). No DLL roundtrip.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.GameOnly">Show only game-specific classes (exclude engine)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.Deep">Also descend into nested struct members and struct-typed container elements (TArray/TSet/TMap of structs), so a field buried inside a struct/array (e.g. SaveSlotList[].MsTuneData.GP) becomes findable by name. Slower; matches show a dotted path and an owning class. Off = fast direct-field search only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.FindInstances">Find in Instance Finder: switch to the Instance Finder tab, fill in this row's class name, and run the search to list all live instances of it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.PropertySearch.CopyOffset">Copy property offset to clipboard</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Game Class Filter -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.Load">Load class list from game process</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.GameOnly">Show only game-specific classes (exclude engine)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.SuperFilter">Filter by super/parent class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.PackageFilter">Filter by UE package name</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.Clear">Clear all filters</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.FindInstances">Find live instances of this class</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.WalkClass">Open class in the Class Struct tab to inspect its full property layout and offsets</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.GameClassFilter.FindFunc">Find UFunctions that take this class as a parameter or return value (reflection scan — includes native functions, unlike the per-field bytecode xref)</sys:String>

    <!-- Live Walker: Functions -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LiveWalker.Functions">Functions</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LiveWalker.FuncGenScript">INV</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FuncGenScript">INV = generate a self-contained CE AA (Auto-Assembler) Lua script for this UFunction and push it to CE (or clipboard if AOBMaker is off). It resolves a live instance + the UFunction through the DLL mailbox and calls ProcessEvent — no helper file needed. Functions with input params pop a CE form so you type values at runtime. The return value is written into the mailbox params buffer; it is NOT shown by default. Set UE5_DEBUG=1 in CE's Lua console (or edit the script's DEBUG line) to make it print the decoded return value and keep the Lua Engine window open.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LiveWalker.FuncPipeInvoke">PIPE</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FuncPipeInvoke">PIPE = invoke this function right now, in-app, over the pipe (the DLL runs ProcessEvent in-process, bypassing CE). Opens a dialog to fill params, FIRE, and read the DECODED return value inline (this is where you SEE the result). Use this to test a call; use INV / AA(Baked) to ship a reusable CE script.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.LiveWalker.FuncCopyBaked">AA(Baked)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FuncCopyBaked">AA(Baked) = &quot;baked&quot; CE AA (Auto-Assembler) script: the param values you enter are inlined at generation time, so enabling the memory-record fires the call once with those fixed values — no runtime form. ~5x smaller than INV and redistributable. Requires ue5_invoke_helper.lua embedded in your .CT via Table -&gt; Add File... (Tools -&gt; Export CE Helper Lua File... to obtain it). Tick &quot;Verify return value&quot; in the dialog for a full before/after dump, or set UE5_DEBUG=1 to print the decoded return value.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="str.LiveWalker.GuessWhat">Guess?</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Live Walker -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.StartWorld">Navigate to GWorld and list top-level objects</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.StartGameEngine">Root the walker on the live GameEngine (UEngine) object — drill GameInstance / GameViewport / World. Found by reflected member, not class name. AOB export is unavailable from this root (direct address only).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.Back">Go back to the previous object, restoring the rows you had selected and the scroll position. Shortcut: Alt+Left, mouse button 4, or the Browser Back key (only while this tab is on screen).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.Forward">Return to the object Back just left, restoring the rows you had selected and the scroll position. Shortcut: Alt+Right, mouse button 5, or the Browser Forward key (only while this tab is on screen). Forward re-reads the object from the game: if it was freed or recycled meanwhile, the selection is dropped and that is reported. Navigating anywhere new clears the forward history, exactly like a browser.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.Refresh">Re-read current object fields from memory</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FindRefs">Find UObjects that hold a pointer to this one (true logical owner — Outer/Parent often only gives the naming-hierarchy package)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.AddressInput">Address to navigate to. Accepts plain hex (0x7FF… or 7FF…) or a CE module+offset such as Game.exe+1A2B3C. A CE-copied, quoted form like &quot;Game.exe&quot;+1A2B is accepted too — the surrounding quotes are stripped and module names containing spaces work. Press Enter or click Go.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.Go">Navigate to the specified address</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FieldInst">List live instances of this field's object class in the Instance Finder tab — pre-fills the class name and runs the search.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportCE">Copy full CE XML pointer chain to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportCEField">Copy CE XML for the selected field(s) only (Ctrl/Shift-click to multi-select)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CancelExport">Abort the in-progress export (Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field / Export CSX) — stops resolving the object graph; the clipboard/file is left untouched</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.AobToggle">Use AOB-scanned GWorld symbol instead of hardcoded address (survives game restarts)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportOptions">Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field options: memory-record Description format (+Offset / +Type) and the AOB / Collapse chain / Guess? toggles.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.DescShowOffset">Append each node's field offset (hex) to its CE Description — e.g. "AbilitySystemComponent (7E0)". Off = name only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.DescShowType">Append each node's class / struct / element type to its CE Description — e.g. "AbilitySystemComponent (LSAbilitySystemComponent)", "[0] (ShieldAttributeSet)". The folded (Collapse chain) spine never adds a type. Off = name only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.DedupShared">Expand each drilled object once: a shared object reached from several fields (or a back-reference) is fully expanded the first time and shown as a flat "(shared)" pointer afterwards. Prevents the huge / out-of-memory export when the drilldown reaches a densely-connected object (e.g. the World). Off = expand every reference fully (can be very large at high Drill Depth).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExcludeSysComponents">Default ON. When DRILLING into pointer/struct children, skip fields whose object class is a system/engine asset (Widget, SoundBase, Texture, Material, ParticleSystem, Niagara, AnimInstance) so the CE export stays focused on gameplay data. The fields you explicitly selected are always kept — only resolved children are filtered. Gameplay classes (Actor / Pawn / Character / components / Controller / PlayerState / GameInstance) are never skipped. Container (array/map/set) elements are not filtered. A "N system fields hidden" note shows when anything was dropped. Off = export every drilled field.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CollapseChain">Collapse the multi-level GWorld-to-target pointer hops into a single CE multi-level-pointer entry (keeps base, one merged spine node, and the target field with its drill-down). Applies to Copy CE XML and Copy CE Field only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FlattenGas">Flatten GAS attributes one level in Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field. A GameplayAttributeData struct (HealthPoint, Mana, …) normally exports as a parent node with BaseValue / CurrentValue children; with this on, those become sibling leaves named "HealthPoint ▸ BaseValue" at the combined offset — fewer nodes, easier to read and edit in CE. Scoped to GameplayAttributeData structs only; other structs keep their nested group. The "▸" segments honour Append +Offset; the struct type (Append +Type) is shown once at the end. Off = keep the nested attribute group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FlattenLeafStructs">Flatten ANY terminal struct one level in Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field — a superset of Flatten GAS attributes. When a struct's whole contents are primitive inline values (int / float / bool / enum) — e.g. a Vector (X/Y/Z), a Rotator, or a plain {Min, Max} pair — its children are promoted to "Struct ▸ Field" sibling leaves at the combined offset instead of a nested group, identical to the GAS flatten. A struct that contains a pointer / object, a string (FString / FName / FText) or a container (array / map / set) keeps its nested group (pointer-backed values are never flattened). The "▸" segments honour Append +Offset; the struct type (Append +Type) is shown once at the end. Off = keep nested groups.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.FlattenLeafRecords">Flatten leaf "record" structs one level in Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field — a superset of Flatten primitive-leaf structs. In addition to primitive scalars (int / float / bool / enum), it also accepts FName and FString (FString / FUtf8String / FAnsiString) fields as leaves, so a save-data record like {Score, Time, Rank, MsID (FName), PilotName (FString)} has every field promoted to a "Struct ▸ Field" sibling leaf instead of a nested group — including records stored inside a TMap / TArray (the per-element groups collapse to flat rows). An FName renders as its 4-byte index; an FString renders as a CE String (one Data-pointer deref). No field-count limit (the all-leaf requirement is the safety gate). A struct that still contains a pointer / object, an FText, a container, or an unresolved nested struct keeps its nested group. The "▸" segments honour Append +Offset; the struct type (Append +Type) is shown once at the end. Off = keep nested groups.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.RecordColors">Open the Record Colors editor. When a TMap / TArray of records is flattened (Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field), each element's rows are tinted by index parity so the records stay separable in CE once the per-element folder is gone. Even-indexed (struct[0], [2], …) and odd-indexed (struct[1], [3], …) get different text colors; pick from a neutral preset palette or a hex value, or Reset to use CE's theme. Default: Even = azure, Odd = unset. Persisted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CollapseLeafPointers">Collapse a drilled pointer one level in Copy CE XML / Copy CE Field when its target holds a SINGLE watchable value. If an Object/Weak/etc. pointer resolves to an object whose only field is a scalar, an FName, or an FString, it is emitted as one "Pointer ▸ Field" record at the pointer offset with a dereference chain (scalar/name: Offsets=[fieldOffset]; FString: Offsets=[0, fieldOffset]) instead of a folder containing a lone child — the "pointer to a string" case. A pointer whose target has two or more fields keeps its group (its object identity is worth a boundary). Off = keep the nested pointer group.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportCsx">Export CE Structure Dissect script (.csx). Pick the CE format version from the dropdown.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportCsxPre77">Legacy format for CE before 7.7: a bit-field bool is exported as a whole Byte with the bit index noted in its description (no bit switch).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportCsx77">CE 7.7 and later: a bit-field bool is exported as a real Binary element (Vartype=Binary, BitStart/BitSize) — a proper bit switch in CE's Structure Dissect.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.ExportHeader">Export C++ SDK header (.h)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CeAA">Copy an Auto Assembler script that registers this object as a CE symbol. On a GWorld-rooted path it emits a RESTART-STABLE script: it walks GWorld → … → this object at enable time (Lua), so it keeps working after the game restarts — AOB-anchored when GWorld came from an AOB scan (AOB symbol ticked), or with a hardcoded GWorld base you update after a restart. Off a non-GWorld path it falls back to a hardcoded absolute address (breaks on restart).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.GuessWhat">Fill gaps between known fields with guessed data types (pointer, float, int, etc.)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CopyFieldAddr">Copy field absolute address</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.CopyFieldName">Copy field name to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.LiveWalker.Search">Search/highlight fields by name. Keywords that return matches are remembered for this session (up to 8, most-recent first) — type a few letters to pick one again. The box clears when you navigate to different data (drill-down / Back / Parent); it's kept across tab switches and refreshes.</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Pointer Panel -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.Copy">Copy address to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.CopyScan">Copy AOB scan hit address to clipboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.ExtraScan">Run aggressive fallback scan for missing pointers</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.TestExtraScan">Simulate scan progress UI (dev testing only)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.ClearGameCache">Remove cached scan hints for the current game (next scan will try all patterns)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.Pointers.ResetAllCache">Rename the entire cache file with a numbered backup (.001-.010) and start fresh</sys:String>

    <!-- Tooltips: Proxy Deploy -->
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Scan">Detect Steam-installed UE games</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Inject">Route ② of 4 — use this when the game is already running and you did not deploy a proxy (① Deploy is better when you can restart the game: it loads with it and survives restarts). Inject UE5Dumper.dll into an already-running UE game (CreateRemoteThread + LoadLibraryW) — no Cheat Engine or pre-deployed proxy needed. Pick the process, then auto-connect. If the game runs as Administrator you'll get a UAC prompt to inject elevated (no manual restart). x64 games only; single-player / offline use only (anti-cheat may block).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Refresh">Refresh deploy status for detected games</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.CancelOperation">Stop the long operation currently running in this panel — Scan, Scan Drives, Refresh Drives, Find leftovers, Delete checked, Refresh, Deploy, Undeploy or Update All. Only one runs at a time, so this is unambiguous. It stops at the next unit of work (one game, one leftover chain, one library folder), so whatever is already in flight finishes and the result line reports what actually happened rather than pretending nothing did.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Deploy">Route ① of 4 — the best one when you can restart the game: the proxy loads with it, survives restarts, and needs no Cheat Engine. Deploy the selected proxy DLL to the ticked games.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Undeploy">Remove OUR proxy DLLs from the selected games — every flavour (version / dinput8 / dxgi), not just the one selected above: that radio governs deploying, while this is a clean-up. A version.dll or dxgi.dll belonging to another program (a mod loader, another tool) is always left alone, and is reported rather than deleted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.UpdateAll">Update all deployed games to latest version</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Force">Redeploy over OUR proxy even when the version already matches. Does not touch another program's DLL. Remembered between sessions.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.ForeignOverwrite">Allow replacing a DLL that is NOT ours (ReShade, Special K, ASI loaders, a wrapper the game shipped). IRREVERSIBLE - there is no backup, and the replaced file is gone. Resets to off every time the app starts.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Type.Version">Hijack version.dll — broadest game compatibility (loaded by virtually every Windows process)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Type.Dinput8">Hijack dinput8.dll — fallback when version.dll conflicts with installer or anti-tamper shim</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Type.Dxgi">Hijack dxgi.dll — for D3D11/D3D12 games whose EXE imports neither version.dll nor dinput8.dll (most UE games on Windows)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Type.Winmm">Hijack winmm.dll — measured importable by 24/24 installed UE games, exactly the same set as dxgi. Use it when the dxgi.dll or version.dll slot is already TAKEN by something else: ReShade commonly installs itself as dxgi.dll, and some games ship their own version.dll. A proxy only works if its filename is free, so this is the spare universally-viable slot (dinput8 is only 2/24). It reaches no game that dxgi cannot.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.SuggestToggle">Show a per-game 'Suggested proxy' column: the proxy you last deployed for that game (remembered by name, survives reinstall); or 'injection' if you've only ever loaded it via Inject; else version.dll (the safe default) with the proxies the EXE can actually load. Advisory only — never changes your selection or auto-deploys.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.Source">Steam = detect Steam-installed games; Scan Drives = search chosen drives for UE games installed anywhere (Epic, GOG, manual, etc.)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.ScanDrives">Scan the selected drives for non-Steam UE games. Partitions on one physical disk are scanned sequentially; different disks scan in parallel. Steam libraries and system folders are skipped.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.RefreshDrives">Re-enumerate available drives</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="str.Tip.ProxyDeploy.CancelScan">Stop the running drive scan</sys:String>

</ResourceDictionary>
